WEBVTT

1
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.123
(TRAIN RUMBLING)

2
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.563
(BRAKES SQUEALING)

3
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.363
(INDISTINCT ANNOUNCEMENTS OVER P.A.)

4
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.683
(♪♪♪)

5
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.009
(♪♪♪)

6
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.883
(♪♪♪)

7
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.163
Have a good evening.

8
00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.603
MAN: Taxi!

9
00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.203
Thank you, Jack.

10
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.209
Not much going on for a Friday.

11
00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.886
Eh, it's early yet.

12
00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.361
(LOW CONVERSATIONS)

13
00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.284
Say, Cal, make that a double, would you?

14
00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.329
Sure.

15
00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:58.851
What, are you in a hurry tonight?

16
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.411
(CHUCKLES)

17
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:02.164
And, uh, one for yourself, huh?

18
00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.249
- I got to make a call.
- Oh, thanks, pal.

19
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:21.331
Therese?

20
00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.209
Is that you?

21
00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.283
(CHUCKLES) What do you know?

22
00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.082
I'm saying to myself, I know that girl.

23
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.971
- Jack.
- Gee, it's great to see you, Therese.

24
00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.963
It's been... months.

25
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.645
Months.

26
00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.280
- Jack, this is Carol Aird.
- Ah.

27
00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:40.283
- Pleased to meet you.
- Likewise.

28
00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:44.487
Well, uh, hey, uh, Ted Gray is
meeting me here, and a bunch of us

29
00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:45.687
are heading down to Phil's party.

30
00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:47.171
You're going, aren't you?

31
00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.010
Yes, I just planned
to get there a little bit...

32
00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.491
CAROL: You two go ahead.

33
00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:51.687
JACK: You coming along?

34
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.205
No.

35
00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:56.890
I, uh, have to make a few calls
before dinner anyway.

36
00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:58.730
I really should run.

37
00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.530
Are you sure?

38
00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:02.808
Of course.

39
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.685
Well, then it would be great to catch a ride.

40
00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.609
JACK: Yeah, sure.

41
00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.802
CAROL: You two have a wonderful night.

42
00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.167
Nice meeting you, Jack.

43
00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:17.368
JACK: Nice meeting you.

44
00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.205
(SIGHS)

45
00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:25.243
All right, well, uh, let me go
make sure the loaf's on his way.

46
00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.363
Back in a flash.

47
00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.523
(SIGHS)

48
00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.243
- (HORN HONKS)
- (INDISTINCT CONVERSATION)

49
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.884
BOY: Come on, let's go!

50
00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:55.849
GIRL: Okay!

51
00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:57.684
BOY 2: You see his face?

52
00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:58.887
(LAUGHTER)

53
00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.883
BOY 3: Wait up for me.

54
00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:02.963
- Let's go, Joey.
- Okay, I'm coming.

55
00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.690
(LAUGHTER)

56
00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:07.571
(CHILDREN CONTINUE TALKING INDISTINCTLY)

57
00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:18.121
(LOW CONVERSATION)

58
00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:20.203
(LAUGHTER)

59
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.489
- (SIRENS WAILING)
- MAN: Want to get a cab?

60
00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:27.563
(BELL DINGING)

61
00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:30.643
(TRAIN HORN BLARING)

62
00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.161
(BELL DINGING)

63
00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:50.723
(♪♪♪)

64
00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:53.201
(SIRENS WAILING)

65
00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.447
(ALARM RINGING)

66
00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:18.405
(WATER RUNNING)

67
00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:20.483
(DOORBELL BUZZING)

68
00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:26.121
(SPLASHING)

69
00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.203
(SPITS)

70
00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:36.604
I like your scribbles.

71
00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:39.080
Yeah.

72
00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:40.884
I've been busy.

73
00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.281
I don't know how you do it.

74
00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:44.771
What?

75
00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:47.526
You look like a million bucks
first thing in the morning.

76
00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:50.365
I'll be down in a minute.

77
00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.281
MAN: So I got the schedules in the mail.

78
00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:58.560
You listening to me?

79
00:06:58.760 --> 00:06:59.841
THERESE: I'm listening.

80
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.650
You got the schedules.

81
00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:06.521
There's two sailings to France
in June and one in July.

82
00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:07.806
Wow.

83
00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.644
So what do you think?

84
00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.963
THERESE: I think...

85
00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:14.121
I think it's too cold.

86
00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.448
- I can't think straight.
- (LAUGHS)

87
00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:18.409
MAN: All right, let's get you warmed up.

88
00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.767
GUARD: Compliments of the
season from the management.

89
00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:22.809
Compliments of the season
from the management.

90
00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:24.849
Season's greetings from the management.

91
00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.004
MAN: Anyway, she's just going crazy
with no other girls in the family but Esther.

92
00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:29.207
THERESE: How is Esther?

93
00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:31.283
MAN: She'll pull through.

94
00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.761
Compliments of the season
from the management.

95
00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:37.885
Compliments of the season
from the management.

96
00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:39.201
MAN: I got to open the floor.

97
00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:40.691
THERESE: See you later.

98
00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:44.003
(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)

99
00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:06.731
WOMAN: Ms. Belivet, you're needed upstairs.

100
00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:09.724
Make it snappy.

101
00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:16.208
MAN (IN DISTANCE):
We got to rearrange this whole display.

102
00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:18.328
Should we do it now or later?

103
00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:20.881
MAN 2: Got to do it now before we open.

104
00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:39.370
(BELL DINGING)

105
00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:42.886
STORE ANNOUNCER: Good morning,
happy holidays and welcome, shoppers,

106
00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:44.849
to Frankenberg's.

107
00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.483
Be sure to take advantage of our
Congratulations, Ike and Mamie,

108
00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:52.365
Inaugural Early Bird Special
in our beds and bedding department

109
00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:54.443
on the second floor.

110
00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.646
And on your way there, you won't want
to miss our brand-new General Electric

111
00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:01.965
television stereophonic display
on the first floor,

112
00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:04.771
just past the haberdashery.

113
00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:06.843
(SHOPPERS CHATTERING)

114
00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:12.689
WOMAN: Oh, there are the trains.

115
00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.002
- They're over there.
- WOMAN 2: Oh, they are.

116
00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:19.007
GIRL: Mommy, please, please, please?

117
00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:28.641
Would you like a ribbon?

118
00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:29.926
- Hmm?
- Would you?

119
00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:53.003
(♪♪♪)

120
00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:58.008
Miss? Miss?

121
00:09:58.160 --> 00:09:59.730
Where's the ladies' room, honey?

122
00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:03.286
If you go back to the elevator
and make a right, you can't miss it.

123
00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:05.283
Thank you.

124
00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:07.727
(CHILDREN CHATTERING EXCITEDLY)

125
00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:24.485
CAROL: I wonder if you might
help me find this doll

126
00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:26.563
for my daughter.

127
00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:28.046
Bright Betsy.

128
00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:31.449
Oh, she cries and wets herself,

129
00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:33.563
but I'm afraid we're all out of stock.

130
00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:34.960
Oh.

131
00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:36.963
I've left it too long.

132
00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:38.610
Well, we have plenty of other dolls,

133
00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.206
- um, all kinds, actually.
- Right.

134
00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.562
What was your favorite doll
when you were four?

135
00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:44.721
Me?

136
00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:47.531
I never...

137
00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:49.523
Not many, to be honest.

138
00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:51.250
(LIGHTER CLICKS)

139
00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:53.767
I'm sorry, you're not allowed
to smoke on the sales floor.

140
00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:55.410
(GROANS)

141
00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:57.562
Of all the...

142
00:10:57.720 --> 00:10:59.768
Forgive me. Shopping makes me nervous.

143
00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:01.644
That's all right.
Working here makes me nervous.

144
00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:03.131
(CHUCKLES)

145
00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:04.884
You're very kind.

146
00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:06.485
Here she is.

147
00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:10.241
THERESE:
Oh, she looks like you, around the eyes.

148
00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:12.687
CAROL: You think so?

149
00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:15.730
What did you want when you were this age?

150
00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:17.962
A train set.

151
00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:21.004
Really?

152
00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:22.650
Do you know much about train sets?

153
00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:25.764
I do, actually, and we just
got a new model in last week.

154
00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:27.206
It's hand-built with hand-painted cars.

155
00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:29.806
It's a limited edition of 5,000.

156
00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:32.851
You might have seen it on the way in
over by the elevators.

157
00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:35.571
I would show you, but I'm sort
of confined to this desk.

158
00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:39.322
- Do you ship?
- Special delivery.

159
00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:41.528
You could have it in two or three days.

160
00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:43.250
They'd even assemble it for you.

161
00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:46.166
Well, that's that.

162
00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:48.243
Sold.

163
00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:52.528
Shall I pay now?

164
00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:53.881
Oh, yes, of course.

165
00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:55.405
Um...

166
00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:05.604
We'll need your account details
and your shipping address.

167
00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:06.921
Of course.

168
00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:10.441
(SIGHS) I love Christmas.

169
00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:13.365
Wrapping presents and all that.

170
00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:17.371
And then, somehow you wind up overcooking

171
00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:19.443
the turkey anyway.

172
00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:23.403
Done.

173
00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:28.248
Where'd you learn so much about train sets?

174
00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:29.481
Oh, I read.

175
00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:31.802
Too much, probably.

176
00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:33.325
That's refreshing.

177
00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:36.131
Thank you.

178
00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:38.601
- Merry Christmas.
- Merry Christmas.

179
00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:45.766
(WHISPERING): I like the hat.

180
00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:49.602
ANNOUNCER: There's a gift for everybody in
our accessories department on street level,

181
00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.843
where you'll find the entire
Parker pen line on sale,

182
00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:56.561
including Parker's new smooth lining 51.

183
00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:59.121
With only one moving part,

184
00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:02.443
making writing as easy as conversation.

185
00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:04.602
See what the sensation of the season is

186
00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:07.371
in our toys department on the fourth floor.

187
00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:08.646
You won't be able to resist
the best-selling...

188
00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:09.921
(ALARM RINGING)

189
00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:21.123
(RINGING STOPS)

190
00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:27.526
WOMAN: You look absolutely divine!

191
00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:28.920
Turn around.

192
00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:30.411
- Please.
- Come on.

193
00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:32.210
- Perfect, wonderful shoulders.
- (THUMPS)

194
00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:33.526
MAN: Move over.

195
00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:34.966
Nobody else can see the screen.

196
00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:36.610
Nobody else is watching.

197
00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:38.762
(CHUCKLES) I'm watching.

198
00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:41.400
MAN 2: I've seen it six times.

199
00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:44.643
Hmm?

200
00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:46.689
Right now, I'm charting the correlation

201
00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:49.127
between what the characters say
and how they really feel.

202
00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:52.363
My kid brother, the movie jerk.

203
00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:54.841
(LIVELY CHATTER)

204
00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:58.083
I'm strictly a beer man.

205
00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:00.004
Everything else makes me want to vomit.

206
00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:01.286
(CHUCKLES)

207
00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:04.091
Well, wine makes me feel naughty,
but in a good way.

208
00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:06.607
I drink to forget I got to
get up for work in the morning.

209
00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:07.886
Oh, see, that's your problem, Semco.

210
00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:10.571
You really ought to drink because
you remember you have a job.

211
00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:11.960
Employment's a curse.

212
00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:13.281
You have a job, Phil.

213
00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:14.487
You call that a job?

214
00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:15.641
I call that an illusion.

215
00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:16.847
You get paid.

216
00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:18.684
Is money an illusion?

217
00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:20.649
My kid brother, the jerk philosopher.

218
00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:22.643
And where do you work?

219
00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:24.564
- Mmm.
- Didn't you know?

220
00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:27.121
Dannie works at the New York Times.

221
00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:28.327
No kidding.

222
00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:30.244
It's a job.

223
00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:32.084
What I really want to do is write.

224
00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:34.163
That's why I watch movies.

225
00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:35.765
Everybody's a writer.

226
00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:39.844
Uh, say, Therese,
before I get too drunk to remember...

227
00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:41.604
(GASPS) You did it?

228
00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:42.761
It's fixed?

229
00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:44.365
He said it was a cinch, no sweat.

230
00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:45.607
Oh, thank you, Phil.

231
00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:47.649
I was missing it.

232
00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:49.325
So you take pictures?

233
00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:50.760
Well...

234
00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:52.410
She's more excited about some chintzy camera

235
00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:54.483
than she is about sailing with me to Europe.

236
00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:56.961
- Women!
- You said it, pal.

237
00:14:57.120 --> 00:14:58.724
- I don't believe it!
- Oh, look who's coming.

238
00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:00.882
Does the House Un-American Activities know

239
00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:02.804
- you two are back on the streets?
- It's been ages.

240
00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:04.769
- Call me, would you?
- I will, I promise.

241
00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:07.844
- You still owe me for that poker game!
- Richard, watch out!

242
00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:09.769
- Yeah, sure, I do.
- Say,

243
00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:13.640
you should come by the Times
for dinner sometime.

244
00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:15.808
I work nights, so...

245
00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:17.689
I got a pal who's a junior photo editor.

246
00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:18.927
He loves to pontificate.

247
00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:20.764
- I'll introduce you.
- Really?

248
00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:23.048
- Yeah, I'd like that.
- (LAUGHTER)

249
00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:24.725
Yeah?

250
00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:26.120
- Okay.
- Okay.

251
00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:28.203
(HEAVY SNORING)

252
00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:48.723
(♪♪♪)

253
00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:06.000
AIRD: Thanks, Helman.

254
00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:07.286
I shouldn't be too long.

255
00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:09.727
HELMAN: Yes, sir.

256
00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:11.609
GIRL: 64,

257
00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:14.764
- 65,
- 65.

258
00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:16.206
66,

259
00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:18.402
67,

260
00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:20.005
- 68...
- AIRD: Hello, Florence.

261
00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:21.366
- That must be your daddy.
- FLORENCE: Hello, Mr. Aird.

262
00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:23.921
- Come on, better finish up.
- AIRD: Is she upstairs?

263
00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:26.162
FLORENCE: Yes, sir. She's in the bedroom.

264
00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:30.646
Mommy, can you come skating, too?

265
00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:32.564
I wish I could, sweet pea.

266
00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:34.927
Why not? Pretty please?

267
00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:37.003
AIRD: Hiya, sunshine.

268
00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.090
GIRL: Daddy, I want Mommy to come.

269
00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:41.765
AIRD: Oh, you do, do you?

270
00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:42.967
Hey.

271
00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:44.201
You're early.

272
00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:49.640
Mail came.

273
00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:50.847
Cy Harrison's wife...

274
00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:53.731
CAROL: Jeanette.

275
00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:55.882
AIRD: Jeanette asked about you.

276
00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:57.690
CAROL: Did she?

277
00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:00.002
Are you gonna color the trunk, honey?

278
00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:02.527
I know she'd love to see you there.

279
00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:09.007
Well, give her my best.

280
00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:11.123
I've always liked Jeanette.

281
00:17:15.560 --> 00:17:17.483
I'd like you to be there.

282
00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:22.361
Sorry, Harge, I have plans.

283
00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:23.931
Mommy wants to give

284
00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:27.368
Aunt Abby some presents.

285
00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:33.681
You've been seeing a lot of Aunt Abby
lately, haven't you, sunshine?

286
00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:35.763
- Yes.
- With Mommy.

287
00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:45.883
I'll see if I can rearrange with Abby.

288
00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:48.042
Thank you.

289
00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:52.568
THERESE (VOICE-OVER): Well, I told the
customer it would arrive by Christmas Eve.

290
00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:54.961
According to what we've been told,
three days should be plenty of time.

291
00:17:55.120 --> 00:17:56.531
It should've been delivered this afternoon.

292
00:17:56.680 --> 00:17:58.444
Oh.

293
00:17:58.600 --> 00:17:59.726
Right.

294
00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:01.445
So it arrived, she signed for it?

295
00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:02.681
It arrived.

296
00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:04.041
Great. Thank you.

297
00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:05.281
Thanks.

298
00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:08.487
- Yes?
- OPERATOR: We have a call for

299
00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:10.125
employee 645-A, Therese Belivet.

300
00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:11.884
THERESE: ...blue eyes, and this one

301
00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:13.530
- has brown eyes...
- Belivet.

302
00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:15.170
Miss Belivet.

303
00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:17.163
Over here, please, now. (SNAPS FINGERS)

304
00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:18.651
I'm sorry. Would you excuse me?

305
00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:20.802
- I'll just be one moment.
- Of course.

306
00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:26.801
Yes, um, I was trying
to decide between the two.

307
00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:29.167
All right. Well, this one has...

308
00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:30.685
THERESE: Hello?

309
00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:33.525
OPERATOR:
Is this employee 645-A, Therese Belivet?

310
00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:35.921
- Yes.
- We're patching you through.

311
00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:37.963
THERESE (OVER PHONE): Hello?

312
00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:40.691
- So it was you.
- (TV PLAYING IN OTHER ROOM)

313
00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:42.325
Oh, hello, Mrs. Aird.

314
00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.403
Did you receive the train set all right?

315
00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:46.244
I did. Yes.

316
00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:47.811
And the gloves.

317
00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:49.086
Thank you so much.

318
00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:50.571
You're a star for sending them.

319
00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:52.444
(RINDY GIGGLING IN OTHER ROOM)

320
00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:55.120
I just wanted to say thank you, really.

321
00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:57.044
Of course.

322
00:18:57.200 --> 00:18:59.282
Well, what I wanted to say was...

323
00:18:59.480 --> 00:19:01.448
do you get a lunch hour there?

324
00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:03.125
Well, let me take you to lunch.

325
00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:04.560
It's the least I can do.

326
00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:06.131
Well, yes, I...

327
00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:07.970
Of course, but you really don't have to.

328
00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:09.963
- I'm free tomorrow.
- Tomorrow?

329
00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:12.003
Do you know Scotty's on Madison?

330
00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:14.885
No, I don't know it. Hold on.

331
00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:17.845
Could I borrow a pencil and paper?

332
00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:26.123
Thank you.

333
00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:29.404
Um, all right.

334
00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:31.483
(♪♪♪)

335
00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:49.251
- WAITER: There you are.
- Thank you.

336
00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:52.450
I'm so sorry to keep you waiting.

337
00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:56.168
I'll have the creamed spinach
over poached eggs.

338
00:19:56.320 --> 00:19:59.529
And a dry martini with an olive.

339
00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:03.765
Um, I'll have the same.

340
00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:05.485
WAITER: The meal or the drink?

341
00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:08.120
Um, all of it. Thank you.

342
00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:13.246
Cigarette?

343
00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:15.482
Yes, please.

344
00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:29.961
So, what kind of name is Belivet?

345
00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:31.406
It's Czech.

346
00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:32.607
It's changed. Originally, it was...

347
00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:34.364
It's very original.

348
00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:35.965
Well...

349
00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:38.083
- And your first name?
- Therese.

350
00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:39.321
Therese.

351
00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:42.324
- Not Theresa.
- No.

352
00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:45.689
Therese Belivet.

353
00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:48.087
It's lovely.

354
00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:50.607
And yours?

355
00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:52.848
Carol.

356
00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:55.810
- Carol.
- WAITER: And two dry martinis.

357
00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:58.088
CAROL: Thank you.

358
00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:03.082
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

359
00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:13.726
So, I'm sure you thought it was a man
who sent you back your gloves.

360
00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:16.963
Hmm. I did.

361
00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:19.242
Thought it might've been a man
in the ski department.

362
00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:20.731
I'm sorry.

363
00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:22.405
No, I'm delighted.

364
00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:25.962
I doubt very much I would've
gone to lunch with him.

365
00:21:26.120 --> 00:21:28.009
Oh, your perfume...

366
00:21:28.160 --> 00:21:29.241
Yes?

367
00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:30.401
It's nice.

368
00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:31.607
Thank you.

369
00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:34.441
Harge bought me a bottle years ago,
before we were married,

370
00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:36.204
and I've been wearing it ever since.

371
00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:38.010
Harge is your husband?

372
00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:39.650
Mm-hmm.

373
00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:42.451
Well, technically, we... we're divorcing.

374
00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:44.085
I'm sorry.

375
00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:46.163
Don't be.

376
00:21:51.720 --> 00:21:54.963
And do you live alone, Therese Belivet?

377
00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:56.849
I do.

378
00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:58.240
Well, there's Richard.

379
00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:00.164
He'd like to live with me.

380
00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:01.481
Oh, no, it's nothing like that.

381
00:22:01.640 --> 00:22:03.051
I mean, he'd like to marry me.

382
00:22:03.200 --> 00:22:04.690
I see.

383
00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:07.764
And would you like to marry him?

384
00:22:08.960 --> 00:22:10.166
Well...

385
00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:13.244
I barely even know what to order for lunch.

386
00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:27.809
WAITER: And your meals.

387
00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:29.405
(CAROL SIGHS)

388
00:22:29.560 --> 00:22:31.050
- Thank you.
- THERESE: Thank you.

389
00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:33.328
WAITER: Enjoy.

390
00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:35.130
I'm starved.

391
00:22:35.320 --> 00:22:37.243
Bon appetit.

392
00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:43.528
What do you do on Sundays?

393
00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:44.761
Nothing in particular.

394
00:22:44.920 --> 00:22:46.331
What do you do?

395
00:22:46.480 --> 00:22:47.766
Oh...

396
00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:50.327
nothing lately.

397
00:22:50.480 --> 00:22:53.086
If you'd like to come visit me sometime,

398
00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:54.685
you're welcome to.

399
00:22:54.880 --> 00:22:59.002
At least there's some pretty country
around where I live.

400
00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:03.844
Would you like to come visit me this Sunday?

401
00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:07.123
Yes.

402
00:23:07.280 --> 00:23:08.566
(CHUCKLES SOFTLY)

403
00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:10.848
What a strange girl you are.

404
00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:13.048
Why?

405
00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:17.288
Flung out of space.

406
00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:22.840
(CHUCKLES SOFTLY)

407
00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:27.683
(♪♪♪)

408
00:23:32.720 --> 00:23:34.927
CAROL: Do you ever put the top up?

409
00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:46.448
(ABBY AND CAROL CHATTING INDISTINCTLY)

410
00:23:47.840 --> 00:23:49.763
(LAUGHTER)

411
00:23:51.320 --> 00:23:54.569
I can just see Harge's mother's face
when she sees me in this.

412
00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:56.484
Maybe I should stop home and change.

413
00:23:56.640 --> 00:23:58.768
Oh, don't be a stupe.

414
00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:04.445
Maybe I should just not show up.

415
00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:09.087
Well, then I'll be blamed for it,
so you better just grin and bear it.

416
00:24:10.480 --> 00:24:12.721
You want to tell me about her?

417
00:24:12.920 --> 00:24:14.968
Therese?

418
00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:17.202
She returned my gloves.

419
00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:18.930
And?

420
00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:22.084
And if you don't get us out
of this traffic soon,

421
00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:25.323
I won't have to worry about any damned party.

422
00:24:32.720 --> 00:24:34.643
(♪♪♪)

423
00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:49.287
CAROL: Oh, where's... my compact?

424
00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:50.771
Goddamn it.

425
00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:53.321
- You look fine.
- (CAROL SIGHING)

426
00:24:53.480 --> 00:24:55.005
- Come in with me.
- Oh...

427
00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:57.322
- Just for a minute.
- (LAUGHS) Don't even start.

428
00:24:57.480 --> 00:24:59.608
You're the one who canceled
on us, you nitwit.

429
00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:01.404
I know. I know.

430
00:25:01.560 --> 00:25:03.050
I'm going. I'm going.

431
00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:05.043
- I'm sorry.
- (LAUGHTER, CHATTER NEARBY)

432
00:25:05.200 --> 00:25:07.043
Call me later.

433
00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:09.646
(CAROL SIGHING)

434
00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:19.371
(PLAYING GENTLE JAZZ TUNE)

435
00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:33.965
You're always the most
beautiful woman in the room.

436
00:25:39.080 --> 00:25:41.242
Tell your mother that.

437
00:25:45.560 --> 00:25:46.925
Keep an eye out, will you?

438
00:25:47.080 --> 00:25:49.082
Cy will scream if he catches me with this.

439
00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:51.129
(LAUGHS) What'll he do?

440
00:25:51.280 --> 00:25:53.248
Dock your allowance?

441
00:25:57.400 --> 00:25:59.164
He doesn't like me to smoke.

442
00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:00.646
So?

443
00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:02.962
- You like it.
- Mm-hmm.

444
00:26:07.320 --> 00:26:09.846
Carol, I... (SIGHS)

445
00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:13.402
It really is not my business, but...

446
00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:17.722
if you're gonna be alone on Christmas,
Cy and I would love to have you.

447
00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:21.123
Thanks, Jeanette.

448
00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:28.531
I don't know.

449
00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:31.166
I might get away by myself.

450
00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:35.767
At least for a few days.

451
00:26:49.360 --> 00:26:53.362
DANNIE: Don't worry, you're not gonna
break anything if you pick it up.

452
00:27:00.640 --> 00:27:02.642
What are your pictures like?

453
00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:06.328
Oh, I don't know. Not very good, probably.

454
00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:08.005
(DANNIE CHUCKLES)

455
00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:09.525
No, I mean, what are they?

456
00:27:09.720 --> 00:27:12.121
What do you take pictures of?

457
00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:13.964
Birds, trees, windows.

458
00:27:14.120 --> 00:27:16.282
Anything, really.

459
00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:18.488
(POPS CAP OFF BOTTLE)

460
00:27:18.640 --> 00:27:20.688
What do you write about?

461
00:27:22.760 --> 00:27:24.683
People.

462
00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:31.200
I always feel funny
taking pictures of people,

463
00:27:31.360 --> 00:27:33.931
like it's some sort of...

464
00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:35.281
in...

465
00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:37.488
Invasion of privacy?

466
00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:39.608
Yeah.

467
00:27:39.760 --> 00:27:41.808
Well...

468
00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:46.324
all of us, you know,
we have affinities for people.

469
00:27:47.480 --> 00:27:48.641
We like certain people.

470
00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:49.966
You like certain people, right?

471
00:27:50.120 --> 00:27:51.201
Sometimes.

472
00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:52.441
And you don't like others.

473
00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:57.167
And you don't know why you are attracted
to some people and not others.

474
00:27:57.320 --> 00:27:59.129
The only thing you really know is

475
00:27:59.280 --> 00:28:03.126
you either are attracted or you're not.

476
00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:07.129
It's like physics...

477
00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:09.641
bouncing off each other like pinballs.

478
00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:12.845
Yeah, but not everything's as simple

479
00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:16.163
as a bunch of pinballs
reacting off of each other.

480
00:28:16.320 --> 00:28:19.290
DANNIE: Oh... (SIGHS)

481
00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:23.126
Some things don't even react,
but everything is alive.

482
00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:42.565
THERESE: It's getting late. I should go.

483
00:28:48.200 --> 00:28:50.202
You shouldn't.

484
00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:53.842
Why? Did you mind?

485
00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:56.048
No.

486
00:28:57.200 --> 00:28:59.168
I have to go.

487
00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:04.923
Come back tomorrow?

488
00:29:05.080 --> 00:29:06.320
- I don't know.
- Wednesday?

489
00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:09.211
Maybe. I don't know.

490
00:29:15.440 --> 00:29:18.683
- HARGE: Here.
- CAROL: Oh. Thanks.

491
00:29:18.840 --> 00:29:22.401
CAROL: Thanks for staying sober
and driving me home.

492
00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:26.281
Good night, Harge.

493
00:29:26.480 --> 00:29:28.209
Carol.

494
00:29:30.320 --> 00:29:33.130
Come to my parents' house for Christmas.

495
00:29:33.320 --> 00:29:34.810
We had a good time tonight.

496
00:29:34.960 --> 00:29:38.009
It was one night, Harge.

497
00:29:38.200 --> 00:29:40.282
I don't like the idea of you being alone.

498
00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:42.124
I'm not alone.

499
00:29:42.280 --> 00:29:45.090
I have Rindy and Ab...

500
00:29:45.240 --> 00:29:46.810
HARGE: Abby.

501
00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:50.607
There's always Abby.

502
00:29:50.800 --> 00:29:52.768
Abby and I were over

503
00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:55.844
long before you and I were over, Harge.

504
00:29:58.240 --> 00:30:02.208
I'll have Rindy packed and ready
for you at 4:00 on Christmas Eve.

505
00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:06.487
It shouldn't be like this.

506
00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:09.843
I know.

507
00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:20.367
My special girl.

508
00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:31.247
Good night, Florence.

509
00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:53.123
Where's this place in Jersey?

510
00:30:53.320 --> 00:30:54.526
In the country, I think.

511
00:30:54.680 --> 00:30:57.001
I don't really know.

512
00:30:57.200 --> 00:30:59.089
My uncle Sal lives in Union City.

513
00:30:59.280 --> 00:31:01.408
He claims it gets pretty
dangerous there at night.

514
00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:03.403
It's not Union City.

515
00:31:03.560 --> 00:31:04.766
Okay.

516
00:31:04.920 --> 00:31:06.763
Oh, there's my ride.

517
00:31:19.160 --> 00:31:20.446
- RICHARD: Hi.
- Hello.

518
00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:21.721
Carol Aird.

519
00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:23.803
Richard Semco. Glad to meet you.

520
00:31:23.960 --> 00:31:25.166
Likewise.

521
00:31:25.320 --> 00:31:28.244
Therese speaks very highly of you.

522
00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:30.721
Well, that's swell.

523
00:31:30.880 --> 00:31:33.087
So you'll get her back safe and sound?

524
00:31:37.480 --> 00:31:39.448
RICHARD: Love you.

525
00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:50.043
(♪♪♪)

526
00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:02.284
I think I see snow.

527
00:32:02.440 --> 00:32:04.329
Wouldn't that be lovely?

528
00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:14.288
CAROL: I love the snow.

529
00:32:17.920 --> 00:32:20.810
Makes it feel like Christmas,
don't you think?

530
00:32:27.440 --> 00:32:29.363
(♪♪♪)

531
00:32:36.440 --> 00:32:38.602
(MUSIC PLAYS OVER RADIO)

532
00:32:56.440 --> 00:32:58.363
(♪♪♪)

533
00:33:13.280 --> 00:33:15.760
If it doesn't blow away.

534
00:33:30.280 --> 00:33:33.011
BOY: Need me to tie this
to the roof of your car?

535
00:33:33.160 --> 00:33:35.731
(CONVERSATION CONTINUES INDISTINCTLY)

536
00:33:43.720 --> 00:33:45.722
(♪♪♪)

537
00:33:49.440 --> 00:33:50.566
(CAMERA SHUTTER CLICKS)

538
00:33:50.720 --> 00:33:52.484
CAROL: Car's just over there.

539
00:33:55.920 --> 00:33:57.604
BOY: Thank you, ma'am.

540
00:34:10.760 --> 00:34:12.683
(♪♪♪)

541
00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:23.324
CAROL: Still with me?

542
00:34:23.480 --> 00:34:25.244
Yes.

543
00:34:29.480 --> 00:34:30.811
(HORN HONKS TWICE)

544
00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:32.962
- RINDY: Mommy!
- FLORENCE: Rindy!

545
00:34:33.160 --> 00:34:35.208
Let's go get your mommy.

546
00:34:35.360 --> 00:34:37.966
- Mommy's home! Mommy!
- Oh!

547
00:34:38.120 --> 00:34:40.851
CAROL: Hello, my darling.

548
00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:42.525
Guess what I brought you.

549
00:34:42.680 --> 00:34:44.444
No. I bet you'll never guess.

550
00:34:44.600 --> 00:34:45.806
RINDY: Is it blue?

551
00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:48.361
CAROL: No, it's green.

552
00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:49.965
Oh, it's long, isn't it?

553
00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:52.122
- RINDY: Yeah.
- CAROL: You need the scissors?

554
00:34:52.280 --> 00:34:53.566
RINDY: Uh-uh.

555
00:34:53.720 --> 00:34:54.881
CAROL: Maybe we should do the star first.

556
00:34:55.040 --> 00:34:56.041
- We can do that.
- RINDY: Yeah, yeah.

557
00:34:56.200 --> 00:34:57.281
CAROL: Where's the star?

558
00:34:57.480 --> 00:34:59.005
RINDY: The gold or the silver?

559
00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:01.527
- CAROL: You choose.
- RINDY: Okay.

560
00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:03.842
(CAROL LAUGHING)

561
00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:05.809
CAROL: That's my girl.

562
00:35:07.880 --> 00:35:09.370
And what do you think?

563
00:35:09.520 --> 00:35:11.682
RINDY: Good.

564
00:35:11.840 --> 00:35:14.411
We need... This one's bright.

565
00:35:14.560 --> 00:35:16.881
- And we can put it right here.
- (CAROL GASPS)

566
00:35:17.080 --> 00:35:20.289
CAROL: That is the most beautiful

567
00:35:20.440 --> 00:35:23.284
Christmas tree in the whole wide world.

568
00:35:23.440 --> 00:35:25.807
(PLAYS GENTLE MELODY)

569
00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:37.804
Were those pictures of me you
were taking at the tree lot?

570
00:35:37.960 --> 00:35:40.327
I'm sorry. I should have asked.

571
00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:42.363
Oh, don't apologize.

572
00:35:42.520 --> 00:35:44.966
I've just been trying to...

573
00:35:45.120 --> 00:35:48.769
Well, I have a friend who told me
I should be more interested in humans.

574
00:35:48.920 --> 00:35:50.763
(CAROL LAUGHS)

575
00:35:50.920 --> 00:35:53.127
And how's that going?

576
00:35:53.280 --> 00:35:56.124
It's going well, actually.

577
00:35:56.280 --> 00:35:58.203
I'm glad.

578
00:36:08.080 --> 00:36:10.367
(♪♪♪)

579
00:36:27.480 --> 00:36:29.608
- That's beautiful.
- (STOPS PLAYING)

580
00:36:31.960 --> 00:36:34.122
(RESUMES PLAYING)

581
00:36:41.320 --> 00:36:44.085
- Is that what you want to be?
- (STOPS PLAYING)

582
00:36:44.240 --> 00:36:46.004
A photographer?

583
00:36:46.160 --> 00:36:47.525
I think so.

584
00:36:47.680 --> 00:36:50.001
If I have any talent for it.

585
00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:53.763
Isn't that something other people
let you know you have?

586
00:36:55.840 --> 00:36:59.208
And all you can do is keep working.

587
00:36:59.360 --> 00:37:01.328
Use what feels right.

588
00:37:01.520 --> 00:37:03.602
Throw away the rest.

589
00:37:03.760 --> 00:37:05.762
I suppose so.

590
00:37:12.120 --> 00:37:14.282
Will you show me your work?

591
00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:15.885
Sure.

592
00:37:16.040 --> 00:37:17.530
I mean, I haven't sold anything

593
00:37:17.680 --> 00:37:20.411
or even shown a picture
to someone who could buy one.

594
00:37:20.560 --> 00:37:22.881
I don't even have a decent camera.

595
00:37:25.360 --> 00:37:28.842
But they're all at my place,
under the sink, mostly.

596
00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:30.923
Invite me round.

597
00:37:37.040 --> 00:37:40.601
(DOOR OPENS, CLOSES)

598
00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:45.562
Harge, what's wrong?

599
00:37:47.040 --> 00:37:48.530
Nothing.

600
00:37:48.680 --> 00:37:51.331
Does there need to be a problem
for me to visit my wife?

601
00:37:59.720 --> 00:38:02.166
CAROL: That's not fair, Harge.

602
00:38:02.320 --> 00:38:04.846
We agreed that Rindy would stay
with me until Christmas Eve.

603
00:38:05.040 --> 00:38:06.849
HARGE: What do you suggest I do?

604
00:38:07.040 --> 00:38:10.284
Do you think I prefer traipsing off
to West Palm Beach for the holiday?

605
00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:12.204
- It was all Mother's idea.
- (CAROL SIGHS)

606
00:38:12.360 --> 00:38:14.124
CAROL: I'm not ready. She's not packed.

607
00:38:14.280 --> 00:38:15.964
She's asleep upstairs in bed.

608
00:38:16.120 --> 00:38:17.485
What about my Christmas with my daughter?

609
00:38:17.680 --> 00:38:19.011
I'm sorry, Carol, it can't be helped.

610
00:38:19.160 --> 00:38:20.525
The flight's in the morning.

611
00:38:20.720 --> 00:38:22.882
And do you think that I've packed already?

612
00:38:23.040 --> 00:38:24.530
- (CLATTERING)
- (HARGE GRUNTS)

613
00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:27.642
Goddamn it!

614
00:38:27.800 --> 00:38:30.451
(PANTING)

615
00:38:43.400 --> 00:38:44.925
How do you know my wife?

616
00:38:45.120 --> 00:38:46.690
Harge, please.

617
00:38:46.840 --> 00:38:49.810
I work at Frankenberg's, sir,
the department store.

618
00:38:50.000 --> 00:38:52.571
CAROL: I ordered a gift from her desk.

619
00:38:52.720 --> 00:38:56.283
I forget my gloves, she returned them,
and I thanked her.

620
00:39:03.680 --> 00:39:05.603
HARGE: Well, that's bold.

621
00:39:09.080 --> 00:39:11.003
THERESE: Can I do anything?

622
00:39:11.160 --> 00:39:13.128
CAROL: Just leave it be.

623
00:39:16.920 --> 00:39:18.843
(SMOOTH CHRISTMAS CAROLS PLAYING)

624
00:39:25.440 --> 00:39:26.566
Okay, snowflake.

625
00:39:26.720 --> 00:39:28.768
Give me a big one.

626
00:39:28.960 --> 00:39:31.440
You're gonna have
the most wonderful Christmas.

627
00:39:31.600 --> 00:39:33.443
I promise.

628
00:39:33.600 --> 00:39:36.570
There's room for you in the car, Mommy.

629
00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:38.524
You can come with us.

630
00:39:38.720 --> 00:39:39.926
No, darling.

631
00:39:40.080 --> 00:39:41.730
I wish I could.

632
00:39:41.880 --> 00:39:43.848
You know, but sometimes,

633
00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:48.728
mommies and daddies just decide
there isn't enough room for them

634
00:39:48.880 --> 00:39:50.803
in the same place at the same time.

635
00:39:52.560 --> 00:39:54.483
Anyway, Mommy has to stay here

636
00:39:54.640 --> 00:39:57.928
and make sure Santa's elf
doesn't give away your presents

637
00:39:58.080 --> 00:39:59.570
to another little girl.

638
00:39:59.720 --> 00:40:01.085
Wouldn't want that, would you?

639
00:40:01.280 --> 00:40:03.362
- No.
- No.

640
00:40:03.520 --> 00:40:05.443
I love you, snowflake.

641
00:40:13.760 --> 00:40:15.683
(SIGHS)

642
00:40:17.440 --> 00:40:18.965
(HARGE SNIFFING)

643
00:40:19.120 --> 00:40:20.804
Mmm, you smell good.

644
00:40:20.960 --> 00:40:22.007
(CAROL SIGHS)

645
00:40:22.160 --> 00:40:23.810
You're drunk.

646
00:40:23.960 --> 00:40:25.166
(SIGHS)

647
00:40:25.320 --> 00:40:27.243
Harge, I'm cold.

648
00:40:28.840 --> 00:40:30.763
I'll make you some coffee.

649
00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:33.246
I'm not drunk.

650
00:40:33.440 --> 00:40:34.566
You can still come with us.

651
00:40:34.720 --> 00:40:36.609
Go pack a bag.

652
00:40:36.760 --> 00:40:38.569
- I can't do that.
- Yes, you can.

653
00:40:38.720 --> 00:40:40.609
We'll get you a ticket in the morning.

654
00:40:40.760 --> 00:40:42.524
(MUSIC PLAYING ON RECORD PLAYER)

655
00:40:42.680 --> 00:40:44.045
What are you gonna do?

656
00:40:44.240 --> 00:40:45.401
What are you gonna do...

657
00:40:45.560 --> 00:40:47.210
you going to stay here
with Abby over Christmas?

658
00:40:47.360 --> 00:40:48.566
You gonna stay with the shopgirl in there?

659
00:40:48.720 --> 00:40:49.767
- Harge!
- Huh?

660
00:40:49.920 --> 00:40:50.921
What are you gonna do, Carol, huh?

661
00:40:51.160 --> 00:40:53.162
- What is the plan?
- (MUSIC GETS LOUDER)

662
00:40:53.320 --> 00:40:54.685
- Stop it!
- Damn it!

663
00:40:54.880 --> 00:40:56.928
I put nothing past women like you, Carol.

664
00:40:57.080 --> 00:40:58.570
You married a woman like me.

665
00:40:58.720 --> 00:40:59.960
(GROANS)

666
00:41:04.080 --> 00:41:06.242
If you don't get in that car
with us right now...

667
00:41:06.400 --> 00:41:07.447
Then what?

668
00:41:07.640 --> 00:41:10.405
It's over?

669
00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:14.803
Goddamn you.

670
00:41:16.080 --> 00:41:18.401
You were never cruel.

671
00:41:19.680 --> 00:41:20.886
(GRUNTS)

672
00:41:21.040 --> 00:41:22.963
Harge...

673
00:41:25.640 --> 00:41:26.641
HARGE: Thank you, Helman.

674
00:41:26.800 --> 00:41:27.801
HELMAN: Yes, sir.

675
00:41:27.960 --> 00:41:29.610
HARGE: How's my girl?

676
00:41:29.760 --> 00:41:32.206
Ready to go see Grandma and Grandpa?

677
00:41:38.320 --> 00:41:40.243
(SIGHS)

678
00:41:43.360 --> 00:41:44.521
(DOOR SLAMS)

679
00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:46.643
(MUSIC STOPS)

680
00:41:47.880 --> 00:41:49.370
THERESE: I should...

681
00:41:49.560 --> 00:41:50.925
call a cab.

682
00:41:54.720 --> 00:41:59.128
Just when you think it can't get any
worse, you run out of cigarettes.

683
00:41:59.280 --> 00:42:00.884
(SIGHS)

684
00:42:01.040 --> 00:42:02.485
THERESE: Oh, tell me where to go.

685
00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:04.722
I'll buy some for you. I don't mind, really.

686
00:42:04.880 --> 00:42:08.486
CAROL: You don't have to run out in
the middle of nowhere and buy cigarettes.

687
00:42:08.640 --> 00:42:10.165
I'm fine.

688
00:42:11.440 --> 00:42:13.204
There's a train at 8:30.

689
00:42:13.360 --> 00:42:15.727
I'll drive you to the station.

690
00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:30.643
(INDISTINCT ANNOUNCEMENTS OVER P.A.)

691
00:42:44.600 --> 00:42:46.523
(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS, LAUGHTER)

692
00:42:54.200 --> 00:42:56.123
(CRYING SOFTLY)

693
00:42:58.200 --> 00:43:00.487
(TRAIN WHEELS CLICKING ON TRACK)

694
00:43:09.440 --> 00:43:11.363
(♪♪♪)

695
00:43:29.480 --> 00:43:31.403
(♪♪♪)

696
00:43:42.280 --> 00:43:44.203
(PHONE RINGING)

697
00:43:54.720 --> 00:43:56.768
Hello?

698
00:43:56.920 --> 00:43:59.241
Do you know what time it is, Miss Belivet?

699
00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:01.323
I'm sorry, it just rang.

700
00:44:03.360 --> 00:44:05.806
Hello?

701
00:44:07.040 --> 00:44:08.849
I was horrible.

702
00:44:09.000 --> 00:44:10.809
Earlier.

703
00:44:10.960 --> 00:44:13.361
Will you forgive me?

704
00:44:14.640 --> 00:44:16.369
Yes, I mean...

705
00:44:16.520 --> 00:44:18.443
I mean...

706
00:44:19.720 --> 00:44:21.848
CAROL: Then will you...

707
00:44:22.040 --> 00:44:25.567
Would you let me
come see you tomorrow evening?

708
00:44:28.760 --> 00:44:29.807
All right.

709
00:44:29.960 --> 00:44:31.883
Yes.

710
00:44:34.840 --> 00:44:37.411
I want to know...

711
00:44:37.600 --> 00:44:39.523
I think...

712
00:44:40.800 --> 00:44:43.041
I mean, I want to ask you things,

713
00:44:43.200 --> 00:44:45.567
but I'm not sure that you want that.

714
00:44:47.520 --> 00:44:50.444
Ask me things.

715
00:44:50.600 --> 00:44:52.523
Please.

716
00:44:56.920 --> 00:44:57.921
(DOOR OPENS)

717
00:44:58.120 --> 00:44:59.281
(LAUGHTER)

718
00:44:59.440 --> 00:45:01.363
(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)

719
00:45:16.520 --> 00:45:18.443
(♪♪♪)

720
00:45:30.920 --> 00:45:31.921
CAROL: Merry Christmas, Katherine.

721
00:45:32.080 --> 00:45:33.923
Oh, thank you, Mrs. Aird.

722
00:45:34.080 --> 00:45:35.241
There he is.

723
00:45:35.400 --> 00:45:36.686
Now will you talk to me?

724
00:45:36.840 --> 00:45:38.683
I just didn't want you
to come all the way down here.

725
00:45:38.840 --> 00:45:41.411
- Oh, just give it to me straight, Fred.
- (DOOR CLOSES)

726
00:45:41.560 --> 00:45:43.961
What am I not to worry about
until after the holiday?

727
00:45:44.120 --> 00:45:47.761
(SIGHS) Jerry Rix served
some papers this morning,

728
00:45:47.960 --> 00:45:50.247
to my complete surprise.

729
00:45:50.400 --> 00:45:52.482
Why don't you, uh, sit?

730
00:45:52.640 --> 00:45:54.688
Why is it people think
you'll take bad news better

731
00:45:54.840 --> 00:45:57.684
if you're sitting down?

732
00:45:57.840 --> 00:46:00.605
Harge has sought an injunction

733
00:46:00.760 --> 00:46:03.730
which denies you any access to Rindy

734
00:46:03.880 --> 00:46:04.961
- until the hearing.
- What?

735
00:46:05.120 --> 00:46:09.647
And I'm afraid Harge has changed
his mind about joint custody.

736
00:46:09.800 --> 00:46:11.040
He wants sole custody of Rindy.

737
00:46:11.200 --> 00:46:14.363
We've already reached
an agreement on custody.

738
00:46:14.520 --> 00:46:16.568
What's this all about?

739
00:46:16.720 --> 00:46:20.001
They're filing papers on the 29th
in District Family Court

740
00:46:20.200 --> 00:46:22.931
for the, uh, permanent custody petition.

741
00:46:23.120 --> 00:46:24.645
Can he do this?

742
00:46:24.800 --> 00:46:26.006
- Is it right?
- I don't know

743
00:46:26.160 --> 00:46:27.605
if it's right, but it's legal.

744
00:46:27.760 --> 00:46:29.762
On what grounds?

745
00:46:29.960 --> 00:46:32.281
Listen, let's wait till after Christmas...

746
00:46:32.440 --> 00:46:34.249
Fred, on what grounds?

747
00:46:34.400 --> 00:46:36.482
They're petitioning the judge

748
00:46:36.680 --> 00:46:39.160
to consider a morality clause.

749
00:46:39.320 --> 00:46:40.810
A morality...

750
00:46:40.960 --> 00:46:43.327
What the hell does that mean?

751
00:46:43.520 --> 00:46:44.965
Okay. (CHUCKLES)

752
00:46:45.120 --> 00:46:47.202
I won't mince words with you.

753
00:46:47.360 --> 00:46:48.930
Abby Gerhard.

754
00:46:50.160 --> 00:46:54.491
Also, they're suggesting
similar associations with...

755
00:46:54.640 --> 00:46:57.120
Well, they're alleging evidence
of a pattern of behavior.

756
00:46:57.320 --> 00:46:59.209
"Evidence of a pattern"?

757
00:46:59.360 --> 00:47:02.125
Abby is Rindy's godmother.

758
00:47:02.280 --> 00:47:04.203
Abb...

759
00:47:06.640 --> 00:47:10.042
If he can't have me,

760
00:47:10.200 --> 00:47:11.281
I can't have Rindy.

761
00:47:11.440 --> 00:47:13.090
That's what...

762
00:47:15.960 --> 00:47:18.008
I'm sorry, but they seem serious.

763
00:47:18.160 --> 00:47:19.969
When is the custody hearing?

764
00:47:20.120 --> 00:47:22.248
It's hard to say with
the holidays and a backlog.

765
00:47:22.400 --> 00:47:24.243
Your best guess, Fred.

766
00:47:24.400 --> 00:47:26.482
Not before March.

767
00:47:26.640 --> 00:47:28.369
Maybe middle of April.

768
00:47:30.880 --> 00:47:32.405
Can I see her?

769
00:47:32.560 --> 00:47:34.085
Not... Let me put it this way.

770
00:47:34.240 --> 00:47:36.686
It wouldn't be advisable under...

771
00:47:36.840 --> 00:47:39.575
- At school? In an office?
- The issue is not where, necessarily.

772
00:47:39.676 --> 00:47:43.287
- Surely, a visit, supervised, but...
- Carol, these are serious allegations.

773
00:47:43.440 --> 00:47:45.044
Forcing contact before the hearing.

774
00:47:45.200 --> 00:47:48.010
You risk inviting further scrutiny

775
00:47:48.160 --> 00:47:50.083
- concerning your conduct.
- My conduct?

776
00:47:52.280 --> 00:47:54.282
Jesus Christ.

777
00:47:54.440 --> 00:47:56.966
I'm her mother, for God's sake.

778
00:48:01.960 --> 00:48:03.883
Morality clause.

779
00:48:05.160 --> 00:48:07.003
I see.

780
00:48:07.160 --> 00:48:08.286
Do you?

781
00:48:08.480 --> 00:48:10.369
No.

782
00:48:12.240 --> 00:48:14.641
There's nothing moral
about keeping Rindy from me.

783
00:48:35.560 --> 00:48:37.483
MAN: Taxi!

784
00:48:54.720 --> 00:48:55.801
(HORN HONKS)

785
00:48:55.960 --> 00:48:57.644
MAN 2: Hey, watch it, lady.

786
00:49:15.280 --> 00:49:17.203
(♪♪♪)

787
00:49:22.240 --> 00:49:24.527
(♪♪♪)

788
00:49:33.320 --> 00:49:34.651
THERESE: Yes, that's it.

789
00:49:34.800 --> 00:49:36.723
Thank you.

790
00:49:40.360 --> 00:49:42.283
(REGISTER BELL DINGS)

791
00:49:52.920 --> 00:49:54.081
RICHARD: Find what you wanted?

792
00:49:54.240 --> 00:49:56.208
THERESE:
Yeah, something for someone at the store.

793
00:49:56.360 --> 00:49:59.011
RICHARD: Huh, that's nice of you.

794
00:49:59.160 --> 00:50:02.209
You should stop by on Christmas sometime.

795
00:50:02.360 --> 00:50:04.169
My mom's sort of planning on it.

796
00:50:04.320 --> 00:50:05.685
THERESE: Christmas?

797
00:50:05.840 --> 00:50:07.330
That's for families.

798
00:50:07.480 --> 00:50:09.403
I'd feel, I don't know...

799
00:50:09.600 --> 00:50:11.887
RICHARD: You are family, Terry.

800
00:50:14.720 --> 00:50:17.326
THERESE: I've been thinking
of putting together a portfolio.

801
00:50:17.520 --> 00:50:20.251
Of my pictures, you know?

802
00:50:20.440 --> 00:50:22.204
Start taking portraits, even.

803
00:50:22.400 --> 00:50:24.129
Apply for jobs.

804
00:50:24.320 --> 00:50:25.890
Maybe even at a newspaper.

805
00:50:26.040 --> 00:50:27.246
Dannie said he knows someone.

806
00:50:27.400 --> 00:50:29.323
Have you been thinking any more about Europe?

807
00:50:31.400 --> 00:50:33.323
Terry?

808
00:50:34.600 --> 00:50:36.523
What?

809
00:50:39.360 --> 00:50:40.930
How many times have you been in love?

810
00:50:41.080 --> 00:50:43.048
(CHUCKLES)

811
00:50:43.200 --> 00:50:45.441
Never.

812
00:50:45.600 --> 00:50:47.489
- Until you.
- Don't lie.

813
00:50:47.680 --> 00:50:49.250
You told me about those other two girls.

814
00:50:49.400 --> 00:50:51.880
Come on, they were...

815
00:50:53.680 --> 00:50:55.250
I had sex with them.

816
00:50:55.400 --> 00:50:57.129
That's not the same thing.

817
00:50:57.280 --> 00:51:00.204
Meaning I'm different because
we haven't gone all the way?

818
00:51:00.400 --> 00:51:01.731
No.

819
00:51:04.160 --> 00:51:06.242
Hey, what's this all about?

820
00:51:06.400 --> 00:51:08.482
I love you.

821
00:51:08.640 --> 00:51:10.722
That's what's different.

822
00:51:18.400 --> 00:51:20.368
Have you ever been in love with a boy?

823
00:51:22.880 --> 00:51:24.530
No.

824
00:51:24.680 --> 00:51:26.205
But you've heard of it.

825
00:51:26.360 --> 00:51:27.600
Of course.

826
00:51:27.800 --> 00:51:29.290
I mean, have I heard of people like that?

827
00:51:29.440 --> 00:51:31.363
- Sure.
- I don't mean people like that.

828
00:51:31.520 --> 00:51:35.209
I just mean two people
who fall in love with each other.

829
00:51:35.360 --> 00:51:37.408
Say, a boy and a boy out of the blue.

830
00:51:37.600 --> 00:51:40.729
I don't know anyone like that.

831
00:51:40.880 --> 00:51:42.928
But I'll tell you this,

832
00:51:43.080 --> 00:51:45.560
there's always some reason
for it in the background.

833
00:51:45.720 --> 00:51:49.202
So you don't think it could just...

834
00:51:49.360 --> 00:51:51.089
happen to somebody, to anybody?

835
00:51:51.280 --> 00:51:53.851
No, I don't.

836
00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:55.126
What are you saying?

837
00:51:55.280 --> 00:51:56.327
Are you in love with a girl?

838
00:51:56.480 --> 00:51:57.811
No.

839
00:51:58.000 --> 00:52:01.004
Don't you know I want to spend
my life with you?

840
00:52:01.160 --> 00:52:02.207
Come to France with me.

841
00:52:02.360 --> 00:52:03.361
Let's get married.

842
00:52:03.520 --> 00:52:05.204
Richard, I'm not ready for that.

843
00:52:05.360 --> 00:52:07.203
I can't just make myself...

844
00:52:07.360 --> 00:52:09.124
What?

845
00:52:09.280 --> 00:52:10.725
- Tell me.
- I'm sorry.

846
00:52:10.880 --> 00:52:12.006
I have to go.

847
00:52:12.160 --> 00:52:13.321
Terry!

848
00:52:13.480 --> 00:52:15.801
I'm sorry.

849
00:52:18.560 --> 00:52:23.129
I found Rindy's hairbrush
underneath my pillow this morning.

850
00:52:23.280 --> 00:52:25.886
Full of her hair.

851
00:52:26.040 --> 00:52:27.371
She does that, you know,

852
00:52:27.520 --> 00:52:31.368
to let me know she's been a good girl
and brushed properly.

853
00:52:33.200 --> 00:52:35.407
Usually, I clean it out,

854
00:52:35.560 --> 00:52:37.961
but today, for some reason...

855
00:52:38.160 --> 00:52:39.446
ABBY: How could he?

856
00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:41.364
- (SIGHS)
- How dare he?

857
00:52:41.520 --> 00:52:43.363
A morality what?

858
00:52:43.520 --> 00:52:46.729
"Clause," he said.

859
00:52:46.920 --> 00:52:48.331
Oh, Carol.

860
00:52:48.480 --> 00:52:50.801
If I am responsible in any way...

861
00:52:50.960 --> 00:52:52.962
Don't you dare.

862
00:52:53.120 --> 00:52:55.441
Don't you ever.

863
00:53:07.000 --> 00:53:09.446
Anyway, I got my eye on this redhead

864
00:53:09.600 --> 00:53:11.887
who owns a steakhouse outside of Paramus.

865
00:53:12.040 --> 00:53:15.643
- (LAUGHS)
- I'm talking serious Rita Hayworth redhead.

866
00:53:15.800 --> 00:53:19.523
Really? You think you got
what it takes to handle a redhead?

867
00:53:23.760 --> 00:53:25.330
You going somewhere?

868
00:53:25.480 --> 00:53:27.289
West, I thought.

869
00:53:27.440 --> 00:53:30.925
At least for a few weeks until the hearing.

870
00:53:31.080 --> 00:53:32.889
What else am I gonna do?

871
00:53:33.040 --> 00:53:36.123
Well, I know you don't like
driving alone, so...

872
00:53:39.080 --> 00:53:41.003
She's young.

873
00:53:44.160 --> 00:53:45.764
Tell me you know what you're doing.

874
00:53:48.240 --> 00:53:50.163
I don't.

875
00:53:54.120 --> 00:53:56.009
I never did.

876
00:53:56.200 --> 00:53:57.725
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

877
00:54:12.560 --> 00:54:14.847
Your landlady let me in.

878
00:54:17.280 --> 00:54:19.521
Merry Christmas.

879
00:54:22.160 --> 00:54:24.083
Open it.

880
00:54:38.600 --> 00:54:41.171
Oh, Carol.

881
00:54:43.320 --> 00:54:45.607
(♪♪♪)

882
00:54:55.400 --> 00:54:56.765
It's not very good.

883
00:54:56.920 --> 00:54:58.251
I was rushed.

884
00:54:58.400 --> 00:55:00.767
I mean, I can do better.

885
00:55:02.640 --> 00:55:04.608
CAROL: It's perfect.

886
00:55:08.960 --> 00:55:10.769
Was this you?

887
00:55:10.920 --> 00:55:12.843
Mm-hmm.

888
00:55:18.920 --> 00:55:22.768
Do you have anything other than
photo chemicals in the icebox?

889
00:55:22.920 --> 00:55:24.285
I'm feeling...

890
00:55:24.440 --> 00:55:26.363
Sure.

891
00:55:34.840 --> 00:55:36.763
(SIGHS)

892
00:55:45.560 --> 00:55:47.801
(CAROL CRYING SOFTLY)

893
00:56:00.240 --> 00:56:04.443
Is there any point in,
I don't know, fighting it?

894
00:56:07.080 --> 00:56:08.889
The injunction?

895
00:56:09.080 --> 00:56:10.923
No.

896
00:56:11.080 --> 00:56:12.605
I feel useless,

897
00:56:12.760 --> 00:56:15.730
like I can't help you or offer anything.

898
00:56:15.920 --> 00:56:19.003
(SIGHS) It has nothing to do with you.

899
00:56:24.240 --> 00:56:26.163
I'm going away for a while.

900
00:56:28.880 --> 00:56:29.881
When?

901
00:56:30.040 --> 00:56:31.451
Where?

902
00:56:31.600 --> 00:56:33.967
Wherever my car will take me.

903
00:56:34.120 --> 00:56:36.407
West.

904
00:56:38.320 --> 00:56:40.243
Soon.

905
00:56:42.520 --> 00:56:44.727
And I thought, perhaps,

906
00:56:44.880 --> 00:56:46.769
you might like to come with me.

907
00:56:53.120 --> 00:56:55.487
Would you?

908
00:56:56.760 --> 00:56:58.489
Yes.

909
00:56:58.640 --> 00:57:00.563
Yes, I would.

910
00:57:14.200 --> 00:57:15.850
You don't even know her!

911
00:57:16.000 --> 00:57:20.085
You can forward any of my mail
to Chicago, General Post.

912
00:57:20.280 --> 00:57:22.089
I've paid rent through February.

913
00:57:22.280 --> 00:57:24.169
I had a little extra...

914
00:57:24.320 --> 00:57:26.049
money saved.

915
00:57:26.240 --> 00:57:27.924
RICHARD: For our trip.

916
00:57:28.120 --> 00:57:29.884
Our trip, Terry.

917
00:57:30.080 --> 00:57:31.366
And now you're...

918
00:57:32.840 --> 00:57:34.490
I don't believe this is happening.

919
00:57:37.000 --> 00:57:38.331
I can't explain it. I just...

920
00:57:38.480 --> 00:57:39.845
What?

921
00:57:40.000 --> 00:57:42.241
You got one hell of a crush
on this woman is what.

922
00:57:42.400 --> 00:57:43.447
You're like a schoolgirl.

923
00:57:43.600 --> 00:57:45.364
I do not. I just like her is all.

924
00:57:45.520 --> 00:57:47.841
I'm fond of anyone I can really talk to.

925
00:57:48.000 --> 00:57:49.923
Nice.

926
00:57:51.760 --> 00:57:53.569
You know what I think?

927
00:57:53.720 --> 00:57:57.242
I think, two weeks from now,
you're going to be wishing that...

928
00:57:58.640 --> 00:58:01.611
She's going to get tired of you and you're
going to that wish you had never...

929
00:58:01.760 --> 00:58:03.171
- You don't understand!
- Oh, I do.

930
00:58:03.400 --> 00:58:04.401
I understand completely!

931
00:58:04.560 --> 00:58:05.561
You're in a trance!

932
00:58:05.720 --> 00:58:06.767
I'm wide awake.

933
00:58:06.920 --> 00:58:08.001
I've never been more awake in my life.

934
00:58:08.200 --> 00:58:09.804
Why don't you leave me alone?

935
00:58:11.240 --> 00:58:12.287
Are we over?

936
00:58:12.440 --> 00:58:13.521
Is that what this is?

937
00:58:14.600 --> 00:58:15.647
I didn't say that.

938
00:58:15.800 --> 00:58:19.441
But why should I want to be with you
when all you do is argue about this?

939
00:58:19.600 --> 00:58:20.840
To say...

940
00:58:22.600 --> 00:58:26.767
To say for one minute that you
practically want to say good-bye

941
00:58:26.920 --> 00:58:28.490
because of some silly crush?!

942
00:58:28.680 --> 00:58:31.286
I didn't say that. You said it.

943
00:58:31.440 --> 00:58:33.727
You made me buy boat tickets!

944
00:58:33.880 --> 00:58:36.850
I got a better job for you!

945
00:58:37.000 --> 00:58:40.766
I asked you to marry me, for Christ sakes...

946
00:58:40.920 --> 00:58:43.844
I never made you;
I never asked you for anything.

947
00:58:44.000 --> 00:58:45.809
Maybe that's the problem.

948
00:58:45.960 --> 00:58:47.530
I swear to you, in two weeks,

949
00:58:47.680 --> 00:58:51.283
you are going to be begging me
to forget this whole thing ever happened.

950
00:58:51.440 --> 00:58:53.124
Richard...

951
00:58:53.280 --> 00:58:55.362
- Richard!
- You have a great trip, Terry!

952
00:58:55.520 --> 00:58:56.760
(DOOR SLAMS)

953
00:59:22.360 --> 00:59:24.931
(♪♪♪)

954
00:59:31.360 --> 00:59:34.125
- CAROL: Did you get the coffee?
- THERESE: Yeah. Coffee.

955
00:59:51.320 --> 00:59:52.890
CAROL: All right.

956
00:59:54.720 --> 00:59:56.529
(ENGINE STARTS)

957
00:59:56.680 --> 00:59:58.921
- CAROL: Ready?
- THERESE: Ready.

958
01:00:10.600 --> 01:00:12.921
("SILVER BELLS" PLAYING)

959
01:00:28.240 --> 01:00:30.527
(♪♪♪)

960
01:00:50.760 --> 01:00:53.001
("SILVER BELLS" CONTINUES
PLAYING OVER RADIO)

961
01:00:53.200 --> 01:00:55.601
THERESE: I could get used to
having a whole city to myself.

962
01:00:55.760 --> 01:00:56.966
CAROL: Mm.

963
01:01:10.320 --> 01:01:11.321
- For you.
- (CAROL GASPS)

964
01:01:11.480 --> 01:01:12.481
Merry Christmas.

965
01:01:12.720 --> 01:01:14.961
Oh, no, you shouldn't have.

966
01:01:15.120 --> 01:01:16.167
Open it.

967
01:01:21.960 --> 01:01:24.281
(LAUGHS)

968
01:01:30.840 --> 01:01:33.081
THERESE: I played it for you,
on piano, at your house.

969
01:01:33.280 --> 01:01:34.327
CAROL: I remember.

970
01:01:34.480 --> 01:01:36.926
MAN: Got that, uh, poached on wheat?

971
01:01:38.480 --> 01:01:39.527
Thank you.

972
01:01:39.680 --> 01:01:41.409
- (CAMERA SHUTTER CLICKS)
- Oh, God, don't.

973
01:01:41.560 --> 01:01:42.607
I look a fright.

974
01:01:42.760 --> 01:01:45.047
You do not, you look wonderful.

975
01:01:46.440 --> 01:01:48.124
Just... stay like that.

976
01:01:51.840 --> 01:01:52.841
(SHUTTER CLICKS)

977
01:01:53.000 --> 01:01:54.365
Do you miss Richard?

978
01:01:54.520 --> 01:01:56.887
No. I haven't thought about him all day.

979
01:01:57.040 --> 01:01:58.451
Or of home, really.

980
01:02:01.720 --> 01:02:03.643
Home...

981
01:02:08.520 --> 01:02:10.443
(♪♪♪)

982
01:02:22.640 --> 01:02:26.131
RADIO ANNOUNCER: Yes, it's a warm family
get-together for the U.S. President-elect.

983
01:02:26.280 --> 01:02:30.681
His grandchildren and their toys
are his primary concern at the moment,

984
01:02:30.880 --> 01:02:35.087
for what would this joyous season be like
without little people to give it meaning?

985
01:02:35.240 --> 01:02:37.527
(TURNS OFF RADIO)

986
01:02:49.400 --> 01:02:51.607
- (INSISTENT KNOCKING ON DOOR)
- (LIGHT SWITCH CLICKS, KNOCKING STOPS)

987
01:02:57.800 --> 01:03:00.804
(INSISTENT KNOCKING RESUMES)

988
01:03:04.000 --> 01:03:05.684
Let me speak to her.

989
01:03:05.840 --> 01:03:07.410
What are you doing here?

990
01:03:07.560 --> 01:03:09.881
You're supposed to be in Florida.

991
01:03:10.040 --> 01:03:11.485
Well, I couldn't do that,

992
01:03:11.640 --> 01:03:15.167
because Rindy would like to be
with her mother for Christmas.

993
01:03:15.320 --> 01:03:17.049
Not that it's any of your goddamn business.

994
01:03:17.200 --> 01:03:18.690
Now go get her. I know she's here.

995
01:03:18.840 --> 01:03:21.605
Oh, you've got some fucking
nerve ordering me around.

996
01:03:21.760 --> 01:03:22.807
And, no...

997
01:03:22.960 --> 01:03:24.041
she's not here.

998
01:03:25.840 --> 01:03:29.009
Well, that's impossible, because she's
not at home and she's not with me,

999
01:03:29.160 --> 01:03:30.321
so she must be with you.

1000
01:03:30.520 --> 01:03:31.521
Yeah.

1001
01:03:31.680 --> 01:03:34.809
You know, Harge, you have a point.

1002
01:03:34.960 --> 01:03:39.448
You've spent ten years making damn sure
her only point of reference is you...

1003
01:03:39.600 --> 01:03:41.762
your job, your friends, your fam...

1004
01:03:41.920 --> 01:03:44.605
Where is she? Goddamn it!

1005
01:03:44.760 --> 01:03:46.285
She's still my wife, Abby.

1006
01:03:46.440 --> 01:03:47.521
She's my responsibility.

1007
01:03:47.680 --> 01:03:51.002
You know, that's some way of showing it,
slapping her with an injunction.

1008
01:03:51.160 --> 01:03:52.400
I'm closing the door.

1009
01:03:57.400 --> 01:03:58.526
(QUIETLY): I love her.

1010
01:04:00.600 --> 01:04:02.762
I can't help you with that.

1011
01:04:24.320 --> 01:04:25.606
(ENGINE STARTS)

1012
01:04:45.440 --> 01:04:47.920
- Carol?
- Therese, is that you?

1013
01:04:48.080 --> 01:04:49.525
- Yes.
- CAROL: Would you be a sweetie

1014
01:04:49.680 --> 01:04:51.808
and fetch me my, uh, blue sweater?

1015
01:04:51.960 --> 01:04:54.042
It's in the big suitcase, on the bed.

1016
01:04:54.200 --> 01:04:55.770
THERESE: Okay.

1017
01:05:03.720 --> 01:05:04.846
(INHALES)

1018
01:05:18.440 --> 01:05:19.487
(SHOWER CURTAIN SLIDES OPEN)

1019
01:05:19.640 --> 01:05:21.529
CAROL: Hey, slowpoke...

1020
01:05:21.720 --> 01:05:22.767
Found it.

1021
01:05:26.920 --> 01:05:27.921
Thanks.

1022
01:05:28.080 --> 01:05:29.081
Everything all right?

1023
01:05:29.240 --> 01:05:31.481
Yeah, I'm just suddenly starving.

1024
01:05:31.640 --> 01:05:33.244
All right. Be out in a minute.

1025
01:05:41.040 --> 01:05:42.883
You hungry?

1026
01:05:43.040 --> 01:05:44.485
Sure.

1027
01:05:45.960 --> 01:05:47.007
Thanks.

1028
01:05:52.400 --> 01:05:54.164
Do you feel safe?

1029
01:05:54.320 --> 01:05:56.004
With me, I mean?

1030
01:05:56.160 --> 01:05:57.730
(CHUCKLING): You're full of surprises.

1031
01:06:02.440 --> 01:06:05.569
I mean, you'd tell me if...
something scared you

1032
01:06:05.760 --> 01:06:08.445
and there was something I could do to help?

1033
01:06:08.640 --> 01:06:10.005
I'm not frightened, Therese.

1034
01:06:19.440 --> 01:06:23.085
RADIO ANNOUNCER: You're listening
to WHPC, 1480, Canton, Ohio.

1035
01:06:23.280 --> 01:06:25.726
"And That's The Chance You Take,"
from Eddie Fisher.

1036
01:06:25.880 --> 01:06:28.360
FISHER: ♪ Perhaps he'll be your true love ♪

1037
01:06:28.520 --> 01:06:31.763
♪ Perhaps you'll seek a new love ♪

1038
01:06:31.920 --> 01:06:33.888
♪ But that's the chance you take... ♪

1039
01:06:34.080 --> 01:06:38.083
HOTEL MANAGER: Our standard rooms come
equipped with stereophonic console radios.

1040
01:06:38.240 --> 01:06:43.091
Or we do have the Presidential Suite
available for a very attractive rate.

1041
01:06:43.240 --> 01:06:45.368
Two standard rooms should be fine.

1042
01:06:45.520 --> 01:06:47.522
THERESE: Why not take the Presidential Suite?

1043
01:06:49.400 --> 01:06:51.243
I mean, if the rate's attractive.

1044
01:07:04.920 --> 01:07:06.445
YOUNG MAN: Can I help you with that?

1045
01:07:06.600 --> 01:07:07.726
(THERESE LAUGHS)

1046
01:07:07.880 --> 01:07:09.291
Yes, thank you.

1047
01:07:10.360 --> 01:07:11.964
It's so cold.

1048
01:07:12.160 --> 01:07:14.766
It's so cold my glasses fogged clear over.

1049
01:07:14.920 --> 01:07:16.843
(BOTH LAUGH)

1050
01:07:17.040 --> 01:07:18.690
There you go.

1051
01:07:18.840 --> 01:07:20.444
Thanks again.

1052
01:07:20.600 --> 01:07:22.364
- Night.
- Good night.

1053
01:07:24.840 --> 01:07:26.569
- CAROL: Don't blink.
- Sorry.

1054
01:07:27.960 --> 01:07:29.325
There.

1055
01:07:29.480 --> 01:07:30.925
(LIGHT, UPBEAT MUSIC PLAYING
ON RECORD PLAYER)

1056
01:07:31.080 --> 01:07:32.491
THERESE: I need lipstick.

1057
01:07:32.680 --> 01:07:35.604
(CAROL LAUGHS)

1058
01:07:35.760 --> 01:07:36.841
(THERESE LAUGHS)

1059
01:07:39.680 --> 01:07:42.411
(THERESE POPPING LIPS)

1060
01:07:42.560 --> 01:07:43.766
CAROL: Again.

1061
01:07:49.280 --> 01:07:51.931
(NEEDLE SCRATCHES)

1062
01:07:52.120 --> 01:07:55.044
(NEEDLE CRACKLES ONTO RECORD;
LIGHT, UPBEAT MELODY REPLAYS)

1063
01:07:59.080 --> 01:08:00.570
Take a look at yourself.

1064
01:08:03.120 --> 01:08:05.487
(BOTH LAUGHING)

1065
01:08:05.640 --> 01:08:06.766
Beautiful.

1066
01:08:09.240 --> 01:08:10.890
(SIGHING): Okay. Next?

1067
01:08:11.040 --> 01:08:14.929
Would mademoiselle be so kind
as to apply to her pulse points only?

1068
01:08:16.360 --> 01:08:18.681
(♪♪♪)

1069
01:08:21.200 --> 01:08:22.406
Me, too.

1070
01:08:25.800 --> 01:08:27.211
Oh, that's divine.

1071
01:08:28.760 --> 01:08:29.807
Smell that.

1072
01:08:33.560 --> 01:08:34.641
Mm.

1073
01:08:36.960 --> 01:08:38.291
To President McKinley.

1074
01:08:38.440 --> 01:08:40.010
(BOTH SNICKER AND LAUGH)

1075
01:09:01.800 --> 01:09:03.325
(GRUNTS QUIETLY)

1076
01:09:03.520 --> 01:09:05.443
(SIGHS)

1077
01:09:08.920 --> 01:09:11.287
YOUNG WOMAN: Can we get those
postcards in the lobby?

1078
01:09:11.440 --> 01:09:13.522
It would be really nice
to send one back home.

1079
01:09:13.680 --> 01:09:14.681
MAN: Yeah.

1080
01:09:14.840 --> 01:09:15.921
YOUNG MAN: Good morning.

1081
01:09:16.120 --> 01:09:18.361
Glad to see you didn't
freeze over or nothing, huh.

1082
01:09:18.520 --> 01:09:21.000
You, too.

1083
01:09:21.160 --> 01:09:23.845
Afraid the coffee's not very good.

1084
01:09:24.040 --> 01:09:25.769
Ah... as long as it's hot.

1085
01:09:27.640 --> 01:09:28.687
What's in the case?

1086
01:09:28.880 --> 01:09:31.565
Oh... uh, notions.

1087
01:09:31.760 --> 01:09:34.240
I'm, uh... I sell them.

1088
01:09:34.400 --> 01:09:35.447
Or I try to.

1089
01:09:35.680 --> 01:09:36.761
THERESE: Oh.

1090
01:09:36.920 --> 01:09:38.604
Lousy coffee.

1091
01:09:38.760 --> 01:09:40.410
Oh, I'm sorry.

1092
01:09:41.760 --> 01:09:43.762
- Excuse me?
- We were just chatting.

1093
01:09:43.920 --> 01:09:46.810
YOUNG MAN: Name's Tucker. Tommy.

1094
01:09:46.960 --> 01:09:48.564
Therese Belivet, Carol Aird.

1095
01:09:48.720 --> 01:09:49.881
TOMMY: Pleased to meet you.

1096
01:09:53.240 --> 01:09:55.208
Mr. Tucker sells notions.

1097
01:09:57.480 --> 01:09:58.527
I see.

1098
01:09:58.680 --> 01:10:02.484
I don't know what notions are, exactly,
but they do instruct us to use the word.

1099
01:10:02.640 --> 01:10:03.687
Says it appeals to women.

1100
01:10:03.880 --> 01:10:06.042
Do you have any lipsticks?

1101
01:10:06.200 --> 01:10:07.964
No. But I do have a sewing kit.

1102
01:10:10.040 --> 01:10:11.485
You don't need a sewing kit.

1103
01:10:11.640 --> 01:10:13.881
I can tell.

1104
01:10:14.040 --> 01:10:18.445
I think we can make Chicago by 5:00
or 6:00, if we get an early start.

1105
01:10:18.640 --> 01:10:19.766
That is where I'm headed.

1106
01:10:19.920 --> 01:10:21.649
There's a shortcut across the interstate.

1107
01:10:21.800 --> 01:10:23.962
Knocks two hours off the drive.

1108
01:10:24.120 --> 01:10:25.451
Two hours? That would be something.

1109
01:10:25.600 --> 01:10:26.681
TOMMY: Mm-hmm!

1110
01:10:26.840 --> 01:10:28.046
Can we stop and get some
magazines on the way?

1111
01:10:28.200 --> 01:10:29.201
CAROL: Mm-hmm.

1112
01:10:29.400 --> 01:10:33.644
I have "Field and Stream,"
"National Geographic..."

1113
01:10:34.800 --> 01:10:35.926
"Popular Photography"?

1114
01:10:36.080 --> 01:10:39.402
Nope. Course not.

1115
01:10:39.560 --> 01:10:41.528
I am doomed to remain without a sale.

1116
01:10:41.680 --> 01:10:43.603
(TOMMY LAUGHS)

1117
01:10:47.880 --> 01:10:50.121
(♪♪♪)

1118
01:11:01.240 --> 01:11:03.163
(BOTH LAUGHING)

1119
01:11:13.520 --> 01:11:15.443
- (♪♪♪)
- (BOTH LAUGH)

1120
01:11:17.800 --> 01:11:20.167
Oh, wow, much better.

1121
01:11:20.320 --> 01:11:22.243
(♪♪♪)

1122
01:11:41.360 --> 01:11:43.727
(♪♪♪)

1123
01:12:01.280 --> 01:12:03.601
(♪♪♪)

1124
01:12:25.800 --> 01:12:26.847
BELLBOY: Is this all right?

1125
01:12:27.000 --> 01:12:28.240
Thank you so much.

1126
01:12:30.200 --> 01:12:32.806
(GROANS) Finally.

1127
01:12:32.960 --> 01:12:34.883
A real bed.

1128
01:12:35.040 --> 01:12:36.565
Heaven.

1129
01:12:36.720 --> 01:12:39.326
THERESE: This fabric.

1130
01:12:39.480 --> 01:12:41.926
- (CAROL YAWNS)
- And this furniture.

1131
01:12:58.600 --> 01:12:59.761
HOSTESS: Good evening.

1132
01:12:59.920 --> 01:13:01.570
Table for two, please, for dinner.

1133
01:13:01.760 --> 01:13:03.364
HOSTESS: Are you staying here at the hotel?

1134
01:13:03.520 --> 01:13:08.128
Yes, it's, uh, room... number...

1135
01:13:08.280 --> 01:13:10.089
THERESE: 623. Mrs. Aird.

1136
01:13:10.240 --> 01:13:12.527
That'll be just a moment, Mrs. Aird.

1137
01:13:25.920 --> 01:13:28.685
(PHONE LINE RINGING)

1138
01:13:28.840 --> 01:13:30.842
THERESE: Therese Belivet.

1139
01:13:31.040 --> 01:13:32.963
(LINE CONTINUES RINGING)

1140
01:13:38.000 --> 01:13:39.240
(OVER PHONE): Hargess Aird. Hello?

1141
01:13:51.320 --> 01:13:52.731
Somebody's popular.

1142
01:13:52.880 --> 01:13:53.881
All from Richard.

1143
01:13:54.040 --> 01:13:55.201
Are you going to get your mail?

1144
01:13:55.400 --> 01:13:56.765
Nobody knows I'm here.

1145
01:13:56.920 --> 01:13:57.967
Were you making a phone call?

1146
01:13:58.120 --> 01:13:59.121
What?

1147
01:13:59.320 --> 01:14:01.209
No. Ladies' room.

1148
01:14:05.480 --> 01:14:07.608
(♪♪♪)

1149
01:14:14.320 --> 01:14:16.641
(NEW YEAR'S EVE BROADCAST
PLAYING INDISTINCTLY)

1150
01:14:29.280 --> 01:14:32.170
(BROADCAST CONTINUES
INDISTINCTLY IN BACKGROUND)

1151
01:14:33.400 --> 01:14:35.801
- Happy New Year.
- Happy New Year.

1152
01:14:45.400 --> 01:14:47.482
(BROADCAST CONTINUES, NEW
YEAR'S EVE REVELERS CHEERING)

1153
01:14:47.640 --> 01:14:49.688
Harge and I never spend
New Year's Eve together.

1154
01:14:53.360 --> 01:14:55.044
Always a business function.

1155
01:14:57.520 --> 01:14:59.204
Always clients to entertain.

1156
01:15:01.320 --> 01:15:03.641
I always spend New Year's alone.

1157
01:15:03.840 --> 01:15:05.251
In crowds.

1158
01:15:05.400 --> 01:15:06.890
("AULD LANG SYNE" PLAYING OVER RADIO)

1159
01:15:07.040 --> 01:15:08.963
I'm not alone this year.

1160
01:15:11.280 --> 01:15:14.530
RADIO ANNOUNCER: And I'm looking up
now, as the snow comes drifting down,

1161
01:15:14.680 --> 01:15:19.322
and I see a pale winter
moon over Times Square...

1162
01:15:19.520 --> 01:15:23.968
as the new year starts its lumbering journey

1163
01:15:24.120 --> 01:15:25.849
into eternity... (BROADCAST CONTINUES)

1164
01:15:31.320 --> 01:15:32.651
(KISSING)

1165
01:15:47.160 --> 01:15:48.321
THERESE: Take me to bed.

1166
01:15:52.480 --> 01:15:54.482
(BROADCAST WRAPS UP,
"AULD LANG SYNE" ENDS)

1167
01:16:10.600 --> 01:16:12.489
I never looked like that.

1168
01:16:22.240 --> 01:16:23.685
You're trembling.

1169
01:16:29.880 --> 01:16:32.008
No, don't. I want to see you.

1170
01:16:44.760 --> 01:16:46.683
(♪♪♪)

1171
01:17:05.400 --> 01:17:07.323
(♪♪♪)

1172
01:17:27.840 --> 01:17:31.049
(♪♪♪)

1173
01:17:48.720 --> 01:17:49.960
My angel.

1174
01:17:53.240 --> 01:17:55.163
Flung out of space.

1175
01:18:04.160 --> 01:18:05.764
(MOANING SOFTLY)

1176
01:18:13.720 --> 01:18:16.200
(PASSIONATE BREATHING)

1177
01:18:16.360 --> 01:18:18.283
(♪♪♪)

1178
01:18:30.720 --> 01:18:32.131
What town is this again?

1179
01:18:32.280 --> 01:18:34.760
This?

1180
01:18:34.920 --> 01:18:37.321
Waterloo.

1181
01:18:37.480 --> 01:18:38.845
(LAUGHS)

1182
01:18:39.000 --> 01:18:40.445
Isn't that awful?

1183
01:18:40.640 --> 01:18:42.961
(BOTH LAUGH)

1184
01:18:48.400 --> 01:18:49.401
- MAN (OVER RADIO): The two men had left...
- (BELL DINGS)

1185
01:18:49.560 --> 01:18:51.050
...Knoxville, Tennessee
last night and the driver said

1186
01:18:51.200 --> 01:18:54.726
Williams, who was in his late 20s,
had not been feeling well.

1187
01:18:54.880 --> 01:18:56.484
- WOMAN: You the folks in 42?
- Mm-hmm.

1188
01:18:56.640 --> 01:18:57.766
We're checking out.

1189
01:18:57.920 --> 01:18:59.445
Telegram come for you last night.

1190
01:18:59.600 --> 01:19:01.887
(OVER RADIO): The exact cause
of Williams' death

1191
01:19:02.080 --> 01:19:03.445
- (ENGINE STARTS)
- will be determined after...

1192
01:19:03.600 --> 01:19:05.090
When did this arrive?

1193
01:19:05.240 --> 01:19:07.481
Early. 7:00, 9:00.

1194
01:19:07.640 --> 01:19:09.165
I ain't a clock, lady.

1195
01:19:09.320 --> 01:19:13.080
(OVER RADIO): The elderly composer's
home was in Montgomery, Alabama.

1196
01:19:13.240 --> 01:19:15.163
(GASPS)

1197
01:19:22.200 --> 01:19:23.690
Where is my suitcase?

1198
01:19:23.840 --> 01:19:26.047
- I put it in the back.
- I want my fucking suitcase!

1199
01:19:26.200 --> 01:19:27.565
THERESE: Carol, what's going on?

1200
01:19:27.720 --> 01:19:29.643
Carol!

1201
01:19:30.880 --> 01:19:32.644
Carol!

1202
01:19:32.800 --> 01:19:35.087
Carol, what are you doing with that?

1203
01:19:36.400 --> 01:19:38.687
What happened?

1204
01:19:39.960 --> 01:19:42.406
Carol, you're scaring me.

1205
01:20:02.280 --> 01:20:05.011
Where's the tape, you son of a bitch?

1206
01:20:08.640 --> 01:20:10.961
How much is Harge paying you for this, huh?

1207
01:20:11.120 --> 01:20:12.645
I'll give you double, triple,
whatever you want.

1208
01:20:12.800 --> 01:20:14.325
I wish I could oblige you, ma'am,

1209
01:20:14.480 --> 01:20:16.084
but the tapes have already
been sent to your husband.

1210
01:20:16.280 --> 01:20:17.645
CAROL: That can't be right.

1211
01:20:17.800 --> 01:20:20.326
My reputation rests on my efficiency,

1212
01:20:20.480 --> 01:20:22.209
- (GUN CLICKS)
- Mrs. Aird.

1213
01:20:22.360 --> 01:20:25.569
How do I know you're not lying?

1214
01:20:26.800 --> 01:20:29.326
You don't, ma'am.

1215
01:20:31.800 --> 01:20:33.848
(GUN CLICKING)

1216
01:20:34.000 --> 01:20:35.604
(SIGHS)

1217
01:20:37.320 --> 01:20:39.561
Carol...

1218
01:20:48.640 --> 01:20:50.608
How could you?

1219
01:20:50.760 --> 01:20:51.761
I'm a professional, Miss Belivet.

1220
01:20:51.920 --> 01:20:53.001
It's nothing personal.

1221
01:20:56.160 --> 01:20:58.766
Let's get out of here.

1222
01:21:04.480 --> 01:21:05.561
CAROL: No, we left.

1223
01:21:05.720 --> 01:21:07.324
There's nothing you can do.

1224
01:21:07.480 --> 01:21:09.130
As soon as we got your telegram.

1225
01:21:09.280 --> 01:21:10.884
ABBY (OVER PHONE):
What did the travel agent tell you?

1226
01:21:11.080 --> 01:21:14.410
That the earliest flight into LaGuardia
is tomorrow afternoon.

1227
01:21:14.560 --> 01:21:15.846
ABBY: Tell me what you need.

1228
01:21:16.000 --> 01:21:17.047
Oh, Abby.

1229
01:21:17.200 --> 01:21:19.202
I don't know how to fix this.

1230
01:21:19.360 --> 01:21:21.044
I haven't the strength.

1231
01:21:21.200 --> 01:21:23.123
(ABBY SPEAKS INDISTINCTLY)

1232
01:21:32.560 --> 01:21:34.244
What are you thinking?

1233
01:21:34.440 --> 01:21:35.851
(SIGHS)

1234
01:21:36.000 --> 01:21:38.367
Do you know how many times
a day I ask you that?

1235
01:21:38.520 --> 01:21:40.329
I'm sorry.

1236
01:21:40.480 --> 01:21:42.084
What am I thinking?

1237
01:21:42.280 --> 01:21:45.170
I'm thinking that
I'm utterly selfish and I...

1238
01:21:45.320 --> 01:21:46.526
Don't do this.

1239
01:21:46.680 --> 01:21:48.489
You had no idea. How could you have known?

1240
01:21:48.640 --> 01:21:52.122
And I should've said no to you,
but I never say no.

1241
01:21:52.280 --> 01:21:56.046
And it's selfish, because...

1242
01:21:56.200 --> 01:21:59.370
because I just take everything,
and I don't know anything,

1243
01:21:59.520 --> 01:22:01.329
and I don't know what I want, and how could I

1244
01:22:01.480 --> 01:22:03.482
when all I ever do is say yes to everything?

1245
01:22:03.680 --> 01:22:05.569
(CRYING SOFTLY)

1246
01:22:20.280 --> 01:22:23.489
I took what you gave willingly.

1247
01:22:30.720 --> 01:22:34.643
It's not your fault, Therese.

1248
01:22:37.800 --> 01:22:39.723
- (ENGINE STARTS)
- All right?

1249
01:22:42.560 --> 01:22:44.483
- (SIGHS)
- (COUGHS)

1250
01:22:54.960 --> 01:22:56.371
CAROL: Thank you, darling.

1251
01:22:56.560 --> 01:22:57.561
ABBY (OVER PHONE): So, how are you now?

1252
01:22:57.720 --> 01:23:01.244
CAROL: Oh, you know. Shattered, sickened.

1253
01:23:01.440 --> 01:23:03.283
ABBY: Don't worry.
We'll figure this out, I promise you.

1254
01:23:03.440 --> 01:23:05.249
I hope so.

1255
01:23:05.400 --> 01:23:06.640
ABBY: Anything else you need?

1256
01:23:06.800 --> 01:23:08.609
No, no, talk tomorrow.

1257
01:23:08.760 --> 01:23:10.000
- Try and get some sleep.
- I will.

1258
01:23:10.200 --> 01:23:11.690
- And don't worry.
- Thank you.

1259
01:23:11.840 --> 01:23:13.763
- Good night.
- Good night.

1260
01:23:15.880 --> 01:23:17.803
(SIGHS)

1261
01:23:21.040 --> 01:23:23.122
You don't have to sleep over there.

1262
01:23:41.240 --> 01:23:43.163
(♪♪♪)

1263
01:24:01.320 --> 01:24:03.243
(♪♪♪)

1264
01:24:21.600 --> 01:24:23.523
(♪♪♪)

1265
01:24:37.680 --> 01:24:39.603
(♪♪♪)

1266
01:24:52.440 --> 01:24:54.681
THERESE: She's gone?

1267
01:24:54.840 --> 01:24:57.286
Early this morning.

1268
01:24:57.440 --> 01:24:59.681
Is she coming back?

1269
01:25:01.960 --> 01:25:03.883
No.

1270
01:25:05.120 --> 01:25:07.088
This is all my fault.

1271
01:25:07.280 --> 01:25:10.045
That's nonsense.

1272
01:25:15.480 --> 01:25:16.561
We should get going.

1273
01:25:24.360 --> 01:25:26.283
- (SILVERWARE SCRAPING)
- Eat something.

1274
01:25:28.560 --> 01:25:30.483
Suit yourself.

1275
01:25:35.920 --> 01:25:37.922
Why do you hate me?

1276
01:25:39.160 --> 01:25:40.969
I haven't done anything to you.

1277
01:25:41.120 --> 01:25:44.010
You really think I've flown
halfway across the country

1278
01:25:44.160 --> 01:25:48.249
to drive you back east because
I hate you and want to see you suffer?

1279
01:25:49.880 --> 01:25:51.405
It's for Carol, not for me.

1280
01:25:51.560 --> 01:25:53.801
That isn't...

1281
01:25:58.160 --> 01:26:00.083
(LIGHTER CLICKING)

1282
01:26:03.040 --> 01:26:06.564
With you and Carol, what happened?

1283
01:26:06.720 --> 01:26:08.643
It's completely different.

1284
01:26:10.240 --> 01:26:12.527
I've known Carol since I was ten years old.

1285
01:26:20.920 --> 01:26:22.729
It was five or so years ago.

1286
01:26:22.880 --> 01:26:24.644
Summer.

1287
01:26:24.800 --> 01:26:27.451
Late one night, my Ford...

1288
01:26:27.600 --> 01:26:30.126
broke down near my mother's house.

1289
01:26:32.640 --> 01:26:35.849
We tried to stay up...

1290
01:26:36.000 --> 01:26:37.525
but curled up together

1291
01:26:37.680 --> 01:26:39.808
in my old twin bed.

1292
01:26:39.960 --> 01:26:41.883
And that was it.

1293
01:26:43.680 --> 01:26:45.603
For a while.

1294
01:26:49.040 --> 01:26:51.327
And then it changed.

1295
01:26:51.480 --> 01:26:53.403
It changes.

1296
01:26:54.720 --> 01:26:56.609
Nobody's fault.

1297
01:27:06.520 --> 01:27:08.488
So...

1298
01:27:08.640 --> 01:27:09.971
What?

1299
01:27:12.920 --> 01:27:14.365
Here.

1300
01:27:16.560 --> 01:27:18.722
(DOOR OPENS)

1301
01:27:28.200 --> 01:27:30.646
CAROL (VOICE-OVER): Dearest,

1302
01:27:30.840 --> 01:27:34.003
there are no accidents,

1303
01:27:34.160 --> 01:27:37.607
and he would've found us one way or another.

1304
01:27:40.160 --> 01:27:42.322
Everything comes full circle.

1305
01:27:42.480 --> 01:27:45.086
Be grateful it was sooner

1306
01:27:45.240 --> 01:27:48.164
rather than later.

1307
01:27:48.320 --> 01:27:50.926
You'll think it harsh of me to say so,

1308
01:27:51.080 --> 01:27:54.926
but no explanation I offer will satisfy you.

1309
01:27:59.000 --> 01:28:02.608
Please don't be angry when I tell you
that you seek resolutions

1310
01:28:02.760 --> 01:28:06.606
and explanations because you're young.

1311
01:28:06.760 --> 01:28:10.162
But you will understand this one day.

1312
01:28:25.520 --> 01:28:27.409
(COUGHING)

1313
01:28:36.480 --> 01:28:38.687
And when it happens,

1314
01:28:38.840 --> 01:28:43.164
I want you to imagine me there to greet you,

1315
01:28:43.320 --> 01:28:45.448
our lives stretched out ahead of us,

1316
01:28:45.600 --> 01:28:48.126
a perpetual sunrise.

1317
01:29:00.680 --> 01:29:05.160
But until then, there must be
no contact between us.

1318
01:29:05.320 --> 01:29:08.005
I have much to do,

1319
01:29:08.160 --> 01:29:11.289
and you, my darling, even more.

1320
01:29:13.320 --> 01:29:16.324
Please believe that I would do
anything to see you happy.

1321
01:29:27.880 --> 01:29:31.441
And so, I do the only thing I can.

1322
01:29:42.120 --> 01:29:44.407
I release you.

1323
01:30:02.640 --> 01:30:04.563
(♪♪♪)

1324
01:30:22.960 --> 01:30:24.883
(♪♪♪)

1325
01:30:42.840 --> 01:30:44.763
(♪♪♪)

1326
01:31:03.600 --> 01:31:05.523
(LINE RINGING)

1327
01:31:06.920 --> 01:31:08.922
(RINGING STOPS)

1328
01:31:11.440 --> 01:31:13.647
Hello?

1329
01:31:16.440 --> 01:31:18.329
Carol?

1330
01:31:34.080 --> 01:31:35.570
(DIAL TONE DRONES)

1331
01:31:35.720 --> 01:31:37.848
I miss you.

1332
01:31:39.720 --> 01:31:41.722
I miss you.

1333
01:31:41.920 --> 01:31:43.445
(SIGHS)

1334
01:31:44.760 --> 01:31:46.125
(COIN CLANKS)

1335
01:31:51.040 --> 01:31:53.168
(SIGHS)

1336
01:31:55.280 --> 01:31:57.601
(♪♪♪)

1337
01:32:08.840 --> 01:32:10.001
DANNIE: Therese?

1338
01:32:10.200 --> 01:32:13.641
You know, these are seriously good.

1339
01:32:13.800 --> 01:32:16.887
You really captured whoever this is.

1340
01:32:17.080 --> 01:32:19.481
Oh, they're just practice.

1341
01:32:30.160 --> 01:32:32.766
You really ought to put together a portfolio.

1342
01:32:34.760 --> 01:32:39.043
Say the word, I'll introduce you
to my pal at the Times.

1343
01:32:39.200 --> 01:32:41.328
There's always a clerk job going.

1344
01:32:48.320 --> 01:32:49.481
You went away with her, right?

1345
01:32:49.640 --> 01:32:51.927
Yeah.

1346
01:32:53.680 --> 01:32:56.081
What happened?

1347
01:32:56.240 --> 01:32:58.163
Oh, nothing.

1348
01:32:59.440 --> 01:33:02.523
It's hard to really...

1349
01:33:02.680 --> 01:33:05.001
Is this because I tried to kiss you that day?

1350
01:33:05.160 --> 01:33:08.481
Because if it is,
don't even think about that.

1351
01:33:08.640 --> 01:33:10.005
You don't have to be afraid of...

1352
01:33:10.160 --> 01:33:12.083
I'm not afraid.

1353
01:33:19.240 --> 01:33:21.322
Let's finish while we still have light, okay?

1354
01:33:27.200 --> 01:33:29.806
Still think you should
put together that portfolio.

1355
01:33:29.960 --> 01:33:32.440
Okay.

1356
01:33:32.600 --> 01:33:35.046
The world and we have passed the midway point

1357
01:33:35.240 --> 01:33:38.084
of a century of continuing challenge.

1358
01:33:38.240 --> 01:33:40.368
JENNIFER: More mashed potatoes, Carol?

1359
01:33:40.520 --> 01:33:42.409
Oh, yes, thank you.

1360
01:33:42.560 --> 01:33:44.688
They were delicious.

1361
01:33:47.480 --> 01:33:49.323
- HARGE: Here you are.
- Thank you.

1362
01:33:49.480 --> 01:33:51.721
HARGE: Thank you, Cate.

1363
01:33:51.880 --> 01:33:54.929
EISENHOWER: This fact defines
the meaning of this day.

1364
01:33:55.080 --> 01:33:58.922
We are summoned, by this honored
and historic ceremony...

1365
01:33:59.080 --> 01:34:01.811
I had thought, perhaps,

1366
01:34:01.960 --> 01:34:05.923
that Marge and Chester
would be back by now with Rindy.

1367
01:34:06.080 --> 01:34:09.803
Marge said to go ahead, not to wait.

1368
01:34:11.640 --> 01:34:14.723
I'm sure that they'll be here soon.

1369
01:34:17.800 --> 01:34:22.568
Harge tells us you've been getting along
quite well with your doctor, Carol.

1370
01:34:22.720 --> 01:34:24.085
And why shouldn't she get along well?

1371
01:34:24.240 --> 01:34:26.163
He's a very expensive doctor.

1372
01:34:26.320 --> 01:34:29.483
Well, actually, he's not a doctor.

1373
01:34:29.640 --> 01:34:31.563
He's a psychotherapist.

1374
01:34:33.320 --> 01:34:35.926
Well, he's very well regarded.

1375
01:34:36.080 --> 01:34:38.606
A Yale man, like your uncle.

1376
01:34:38.760 --> 01:34:40.603
Yes, but that doesn't make him a doctor.

1377
01:34:40.760 --> 01:34:44.731
EISENHOWER: ...and their vast
empires have disappeared.

1378
01:34:44.880 --> 01:34:48.726
New nations have been born.

1379
01:34:48.880 --> 01:34:50.689
For our own country, it has been a time...

1380
01:34:50.840 --> 01:34:52.524
But I do like him very much.

1381
01:34:52.680 --> 01:34:55.001
He's been a great help.

1382
01:34:55.200 --> 01:34:57.089
(CAR APPROACHING)

1383
01:34:59.200 --> 01:35:01.043
(GASPS)

1384
01:35:01.200 --> 01:35:03.441
- My baby!
- Mommy!

1385
01:35:03.600 --> 01:35:05.682
Oh!

1386
01:35:05.840 --> 01:35:07.922
Look how much you've grown.

1387
01:35:08.080 --> 01:35:10.367
Oh!

1388
01:35:10.520 --> 01:35:12.443
Can you give me a big squeeze?

1389
01:35:14.840 --> 01:35:17.047
Oh, that's a long trip.

1390
01:35:17.200 --> 01:35:20.044
You must be hungry, sweetheart.

1391
01:35:20.200 --> 01:35:22.009
Want to come have something to eat?

1392
01:35:22.160 --> 01:35:23.446
RINDY: Yes.

1393
01:35:23.640 --> 01:35:25.563
CAROL: Mm-hmm.

1394
01:35:43.120 --> 01:35:45.043
(♪♪♪)

1395
01:36:02.960 --> 01:36:04.883
(♪♪♪)

1396
01:36:21.200 --> 01:36:23.282
(♪♪♪)

1397
01:36:31.360 --> 01:36:33.283
Stay, stay.

1398
01:36:33.440 --> 01:36:35.363
Oh, don't be daft.

1399
01:36:45.320 --> 01:36:48.130
I don't think I can keep this up, Abby.

1400
01:36:53.160 --> 01:36:57.165
(CHUCKLES): I mean, how many
more tomato aspic lunches...

1401
01:36:57.360 --> 01:36:59.044
(CAROL SIGHS)

1402
01:36:59.240 --> 01:37:00.480
You know...

1403
01:37:00.640 --> 01:37:03.371
then I come home, every night,

1404
01:37:03.520 --> 01:37:05.363
without her.

1405
01:37:05.520 --> 01:37:07.204
To what? To this.

1406
01:37:07.360 --> 01:37:09.010
(CAROL SIGHS)

1407
01:37:09.160 --> 01:37:10.730
And Therese?

1408
01:37:17.400 --> 01:37:19.243
What about her?

1409
01:37:19.400 --> 01:37:22.006
Have you heard anything?

1410
01:37:23.840 --> 01:37:25.842
(SIGHS): No.

1411
01:37:26.000 --> 01:37:27.764
No, no.

1412
01:37:29.320 --> 01:37:32.483
Been over a month since she tried to call.

1413
01:37:32.640 --> 01:37:34.961
(TREMBLING INHALE) There's nothing.

1414
01:37:38.720 --> 01:37:40.848
I wish... (EXHALES)

1415
01:37:41.920 --> 01:37:43.604
Have you

1416
01:37:43.760 --> 01:37:46.525
- hear something?
- From Therese? No.

1417
01:37:48.920 --> 01:37:52.242
She must have started her job
at the Times, though.

1418
01:37:59.200 --> 01:38:02.283
I should have said, "Therese...

1419
01:38:02.440 --> 01:38:04.169
(STAMMERS SOFTLY)

1420
01:38:04.320 --> 01:38:06.971
...wait."

1421
01:38:11.360 --> 01:38:14.523
CAROL: Who the hell's
turning around in my drive?

1422
01:38:15.920 --> 01:38:17.649
I should go.

1423
01:38:17.800 --> 01:38:19.848
No. No, you don't...

1424
01:38:20.000 --> 01:38:21.843
ABBY (WHISPERS): I do.

1425
01:38:22.000 --> 01:38:24.048
(ENGINE IDLING)

1426
01:38:30.920 --> 01:38:34.049
(♪♪♪)

1427
01:38:53.960 --> 01:38:56.008
(♪♪♪)

1428
01:39:05.600 --> 01:39:08.251
(HORNS HONKING IN DISTANCE)

1429
01:39:11.520 --> 01:39:13.568
(ELEVATOR BELL DINGS)

1430
01:39:22.600 --> 01:39:26.685
JERRY (VOICE-OVER): We expect,
given the seriousness of the charges,

1431
01:39:26.840 --> 01:39:28.968
and the incontestability of the evidence,

1432
01:39:29.120 --> 01:39:32.044
that the court will grant
sole custody of the child

1433
01:39:32.200 --> 01:39:34.965
- to my client.
- Not so fast, Jerry.

1434
01:39:35.120 --> 01:39:38.124
My client's psychotherapist
is perfectly satisfied

1435
01:39:38.280 --> 01:39:41.170
with her recovery
from the events of the winter,

1436
01:39:41.360 --> 01:39:44.486
asserting she is more than capable
of caring for her own child.

1437
01:39:44.640 --> 01:39:48.641
She's had no further contact with the...
the girl in question,

1438
01:39:48.800 --> 01:39:52.922
and we have sworn depositions from two
Saddlebrook Institute psychiatrists

1439
01:39:53.120 --> 01:39:54.849
clearly stating that in their opinions,

1440
01:39:55.040 --> 01:39:57.850
a series of events precipitated
by my client's husband

1441
01:39:58.040 --> 01:40:00.771
drove her to suffer an
emotional break which resulted

1442
01:40:00.960 --> 01:40:02.928
- in the presumed aberrant behav...
- That's absurd!

1443
01:40:03.080 --> 01:40:04.684
- Furthermore, given the way these tapes...
- All right, Fred...

1444
01:40:04.880 --> 01:40:06.530
- ...were obtained
- ...if this is how you're gonna handle this...

1445
01:40:06.720 --> 01:40:08.370
and recorded,
we're confident of their inadmissibility.

1446
01:40:08.520 --> 01:40:10.249
- Fred, please don't.
- JERRY: All right, first off, Fred,

1447
01:40:10.400 --> 01:40:12.323
I want to see these
depositions, and secondly, I...

1448
01:40:12.480 --> 01:40:14.164
May I speak?

1449
01:40:14.320 --> 01:40:15.606
(MAN CLEARS THROAT)

1450
01:40:15.760 --> 01:40:18.281
I won't deny the truth of what's
contained in those tapes...

1451
01:40:18.480 --> 01:40:19.641
FRED: This is off the record, honey.

1452
01:40:19.800 --> 01:40:23.691
CAROL: May as well be on the record.

1453
01:40:23.840 --> 01:40:25.490
Harge...

1454
01:40:25.640 --> 01:40:26.971
I want you...

1455
01:40:27.120 --> 01:40:29.043
to be happy.

1456
01:40:29.240 --> 01:40:30.810
I didn't give you that.

1457
01:40:30.960 --> 01:40:32.769
I... I failed you.

1458
01:40:32.920 --> 01:40:36.720
I mean, we both could have given more, but...

1459
01:40:36.880 --> 01:40:39.406
we gave each other Rindy,

1460
01:40:39.560 --> 01:40:43.360
and that is the most breathtaking,

1461
01:40:43.520 --> 01:40:45.727
the most generous of gifts.

1462
01:40:45.920 --> 01:40:49.402
So why are we spending so much time

1463
01:40:49.560 --> 01:40:52.086
trying to keep her from each other?

1464
01:40:53.960 --> 01:40:56.008
Now, what happened with Therese...

1465
01:40:56.160 --> 01:40:57.525
I wanted.

1466
01:40:57.680 --> 01:41:00.763
And I will not deny it,

1467
01:41:00.920 --> 01:41:03.207
or say that I...

1468
01:41:03.400 --> 01:41:04.970
But I do regret,

1469
01:41:05.120 --> 01:41:08.090
and I grieve for the mess
we are about to make

1470
01:41:08.280 --> 01:41:10.282
of our child's life.

1471
01:41:10.440 --> 01:41:13.489
We, Harge, are both responsible.

1472
01:41:15.640 --> 01:41:18.883
So I think I... we should set it right.

1473
01:41:23.320 --> 01:41:25.129
Now, I think that Harge

1474
01:41:25.280 --> 01:41:29.003
- should have custody of Rindy...
- FRED: Could I suggest we just take a break

1475
01:41:29.160 --> 01:41:30.525
- for a moment?
- CAROL: No!

1476
01:41:30.720 --> 01:41:32.051
Fred, will you let me speak?

1477
01:41:32.200 --> 01:41:36.081
Because if you don't,
I will not be able to cope.

1478
01:41:37.440 --> 01:41:38.601
Now, I'm no martyr.

1479
01:41:38.760 --> 01:41:40.808
I have no clue what is best for me.

1480
01:41:40.960 --> 01:41:44.891
But I do know, and I feel it in my bones,
what is best for my daughter.

1481
01:41:45.040 --> 01:41:46.246
(TREMBLING BREATH)

1482
01:41:46.440 --> 01:41:47.487
(SNIFFLES SOFTLY)

1483
01:41:47.640 --> 01:41:49.369
Now, I want visits with her, Harge.

1484
01:41:49.520 --> 01:41:51.010
I don't care if they're supervised.

1485
01:41:51.160 --> 01:41:53.208
I just want them to be regular.

1486
01:41:55.560 --> 01:41:57.642
Now, there was a time...

1487
01:41:57.800 --> 01:42:01.967
when I would have done almost anything,
I would have locked myself away...

1488
01:42:02.120 --> 01:42:04.600
(SNIFFLES, PANTS) to keep Rindy with me.

1489
01:42:04.760 --> 01:42:06.603
(SNIFFLES)

1490
01:42:06.760 --> 01:42:08.410
But what use am I to her...

1491
01:42:08.560 --> 01:42:11.928
to us, if I'm living against my own grain?

1492
01:42:12.080 --> 01:42:14.890
(SIGHS, SNIFFLES) So that's the deal.

1493
01:42:15.040 --> 01:42:17.691
I won't... I cannot negotiate anymore.

1494
01:42:17.840 --> 01:42:19.683
You take it or leave it.

1495
01:42:19.840 --> 01:42:23.083
But if you leave it, we go to court.

1496
01:42:23.240 --> 01:42:27.006
And if we go to court, it'll get ugly.

1497
01:42:29.240 --> 01:42:32.722
(CRYING): And we're not ugly people, Harge.

1498
01:42:32.880 --> 01:42:35.087
(SOBBING SOFTLY)

1499
01:42:35.280 --> 01:42:37.806
- (♪♪♪)
- FRED: Hey, Carol...

1500
01:42:37.960 --> 01:42:39.166
Excuse me. Carol?

1501
01:42:40.520 --> 01:42:42.249
(CAR HORN HONKS)

1502
01:42:42.400 --> 01:42:45.927
(♪♪♪)

1503
01:42:50.280 --> 01:42:52.601
(♪♪♪)

1504
01:42:58.960 --> 01:43:00.291
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

1505
01:43:00.440 --> 01:43:03.011
MAN: Right there, right there. Right here.

1506
01:43:03.160 --> 01:43:05.322
MAN 2: Guys, guys, pay attention.

1507
01:43:05.520 --> 01:43:07.045
Here, you got the Rooster, you got...

1508
01:43:07.200 --> 01:43:09.806
you got DiMaggio... front cover. Front cover.

1509
01:43:09.960 --> 01:43:12.088
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

1510
01:43:14.040 --> 01:43:16.361
(INDISTINCT CHATTER CONTINUES)

1511
01:43:19.400 --> 01:43:22.085
- This guy, this guy, this guy.
- That's the guy.

1512
01:43:22.240 --> 01:43:24.481
Where's that one we... where's
that photo we had earlier?

1513
01:43:24.640 --> 01:43:25.971
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

1514
01:43:26.120 --> 01:43:27.804
(INDISTINCT CHATTER CONTINUES)

1515
01:43:28.000 --> 01:43:29.331
- I don't know.
- I'm not sure, I'm not sure.

1516
01:43:29.480 --> 01:43:31.687
- No, no, no, no.
- I like this one better.

1517
01:43:31.880 --> 01:43:34.201
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

1518
01:43:37.160 --> 01:43:38.446
Belivet.

1519
01:43:38.640 --> 01:43:39.721
(INDISTINCT CHATTER CONTINUES)

1520
01:43:39.880 --> 01:43:41.609
MAN: The lady is fantastic.

1521
01:43:41.760 --> 01:43:44.161
(INDISTINCT CHATTER CONTINUES)

1522
01:43:44.320 --> 01:43:46.800
Hand-delivered. Swank.

1523
01:43:46.960 --> 01:43:49.247
(INDISTINCT CHATTER CONTINUES)

1524
01:43:57.360 --> 01:43:59.442
(♪♪♪)

1525
01:43:59.600 --> 01:44:02.809
(INDISTINCT CHATTER
CONTINUES IN OTHER ROOM)

1526
01:44:22.320 --> 01:44:23.606
Of course.

1527
01:44:23.760 --> 01:44:25.683
Thank you. Good-bye.

1528
01:44:38.000 --> 01:44:40.526
(QUIET SIGH)

1529
01:44:42.440 --> 01:44:45.364
- (SOFT JAZZ PLAYING)
- (LOW, INDISTINCT CHATTER)

1530
01:44:47.560 --> 01:44:50.325
(INDISTINCT CHATTER, LAUGHING IN DISTANCE)

1531
01:44:54.640 --> 01:44:57.086
I wasn't sure you'd come.

1532
01:44:57.240 --> 01:44:59.561
It's nice of you to see me.

1533
01:44:59.760 --> 01:45:01.683
Don't say that.

1534
01:45:04.480 --> 01:45:06.448
Do you hate me, Therese?

1535
01:45:06.640 --> 01:45:08.688
No. How could I hate you?

1536
01:45:16.680 --> 01:45:19.684
Abby tells me you're thriving.

1537
01:45:19.840 --> 01:45:23.287
You have no idea how pleased I am for you.

1538
01:45:23.440 --> 01:45:27.684
And you look very fine, you know?

1539
01:45:27.840 --> 01:45:30.286
As if you've suddenly blossomed.

1540
01:45:31.720 --> 01:45:34.007
Is that what comes of getting away from me?

1541
01:45:34.160 --> 01:45:35.571
No.

1542
01:45:46.960 --> 01:45:48.121
What?

1543
01:45:48.280 --> 01:45:49.645
Nothing.

1544
01:45:54.680 --> 01:45:58.401
Harge and I are selling the house.

1545
01:45:58.560 --> 01:46:02.121
- I've taken an apartment on Madison Avenue.
- No, thank you.

1546
01:46:02.320 --> 01:46:04.687
And a job, believe it or not.

1547
01:46:04.840 --> 01:46:06.922
I'm gonna work at a furniture house

1548
01:46:07.120 --> 01:46:09.441
on 4th Avenue as a... buyer.

1549
01:46:14.520 --> 01:46:16.841
Have you seen Rindy?

1550
01:46:17.000 --> 01:46:19.002
Once or twice.

1551
01:46:20.360 --> 01:46:22.203
She's living with Harge, for now.

1552
01:46:22.360 --> 01:46:24.089
It's...

1553
01:46:24.240 --> 01:46:26.208
(SIGHS): ...the right thing.

1554
01:46:31.960 --> 01:46:34.566
Anyway...

1555
01:46:34.720 --> 01:46:36.404
the...

1556
01:46:36.560 --> 01:46:39.006
apartment's a nice big one.

1557
01:46:39.160 --> 01:46:41.208
It's big enough for two.

1558
01:46:41.400 --> 01:46:44.643
I was hoping you might like
to come live with me,

1559
01:46:44.800 --> 01:46:47.326
but I guess you won't. (LAUGHS SOFTLY)

1560
01:46:50.160 --> 01:46:52.162
Would you?

1561
01:46:56.640 --> 01:46:58.847
No, I don't think so.

1562
01:46:59.960 --> 01:47:03.246
I'm meeting some people at the, uh...

1563
01:47:03.400 --> 01:47:05.846
Oak Room at 9:00,

1564
01:47:06.000 --> 01:47:08.207
if you want to have dinner.

1565
01:47:08.360 --> 01:47:11.887
If you change your mind,
I think you'd like them.

1566
01:47:12.040 --> 01:47:14.361
(SOFT JAZZ CONTINUES)

1567
01:47:20.520 --> 01:47:21.965
Well...

1568
01:47:24.080 --> 01:47:26.003
...that's that.

1569
01:47:40.120 --> 01:47:42.805
I love you.

1570
01:47:42.960 --> 01:47:45.327
JACK: Therese? Is that you?

1571
01:47:47.680 --> 01:47:49.125
(CHUCKLES) What do you know?

1572
01:47:49.280 --> 01:47:51.601
I'm saying to myself, I know that girl.

1573
01:47:51.760 --> 01:47:53.683
- Jack.
- Gee, it's great to see you, Therese.

1574
01:47:53.840 --> 01:47:56.241
- It's been... months.
- Months.

1575
01:47:56.400 --> 01:47:58.562
- Jack, this is Carol Aird.
- Ah.

1576
01:47:58.720 --> 01:48:01.121
- JACK: Pleased to meet you.
- Likewise.

1577
01:48:01.280 --> 01:48:03.408
Well, hey, uh, Ted Gray's meeting me here,

1578
01:48:03.560 --> 01:48:06.086
and a bunch of us are heading
down to Phil's party.

1579
01:48:06.280 --> 01:48:07.850
You're going, aren't you?

1580
01:48:08.000 --> 01:48:10.401
- Yes, I just planned to get there...
- You two go ahead.

1581
01:48:10.560 --> 01:48:12.449
You coming along?

1582
01:48:12.600 --> 01:48:14.045
No. No.

1583
01:48:14.200 --> 01:48:17.409
I have to make a few calls
before dinner anyway.

1584
01:48:17.560 --> 01:48:20.006
I really should run.

1585
01:48:20.160 --> 01:48:21.730
Are you sure?

1586
01:48:21.920 --> 01:48:24.002
Of course.

1587
01:48:26.600 --> 01:48:28.762
Well, it would be great to catch a ride.

1588
01:48:28.920 --> 01:48:30.684
Yeah, sure.

1589
01:48:33.560 --> 01:48:35.562
You two have a wonderful night.

1590
01:48:35.720 --> 01:48:38.610
- Nice meeting you, Jack.
- Nice meeting you.

1591
01:48:40.960 --> 01:48:42.849
All right, well, uh...

1592
01:48:43.000 --> 01:48:45.810
let me go make sure the loaf's on his way.

1593
01:48:45.960 --> 01:48:47.724
Back in a flash.

1594
01:48:49.320 --> 01:48:51.322
(♪♪♪)

1595
01:49:00.240 --> 01:49:02.083
(WATER SPLASHES)

1596
01:49:02.240 --> 01:49:03.924
(EXHALES SOFTLY)

1597
01:49:04.080 --> 01:49:06.401
(♪♪♪)

1598
01:49:15.080 --> 01:49:16.730
MAN: He just snuck me in.
I saw a doubleheader.

1599
01:49:16.880 --> 01:49:18.245
- It was just fantastic.
- WOMAN: Is that right?

1600
01:49:18.400 --> 01:49:20.129
Yeah, great.

1601
01:49:20.280 --> 01:49:21.361
(♪♪♪)

1602
01:49:21.560 --> 01:49:23.050
MAN: You know, his brother, uh...

1603
01:49:23.200 --> 01:49:24.884
(MAN CONTINUES INDISTINCTLY)

1604
01:49:25.040 --> 01:49:27.441
...yeah, he snuck in.
Anyway, he had the right idea.

1605
01:49:27.600 --> 01:49:29.762
(CONVERSATION CONTINUES INDISTINCTLY)

1606
01:49:29.920 --> 01:49:32.002
Yes, right up here,
right up here at the corner.

1607
01:49:32.200 --> 01:49:34.726
(HORN HONKING REPEATEDLY)

1608
01:49:39.320 --> 01:49:41.163
(♪♪♪)

1609
01:49:41.320 --> 01:49:43.891
- PHIL: It's about time, Belivet!
- Hey, keep the change.

1610
01:49:44.040 --> 01:49:46.441
Say hello, at least.
It hasn't been that long.

1611
01:49:46.600 --> 01:49:48.967
- THERESE: Hello, Phil. Sorry.
- DANNIE: Oh, there she is!

1612
01:49:49.120 --> 01:49:50.565
Get up there!

1613
01:49:50.760 --> 01:49:52.171
What?

1614
01:49:52.320 --> 01:49:56.803
Nothing. There better be beer
up there, or wine.

1615
01:49:56.960 --> 01:49:58.644
(INDISTINCT CHATTER, LAUGHING)

1616
01:49:58.800 --> 01:50:00.962
(♪♪♪)

1617
01:50:17.360 --> 01:50:19.362
(♪♪♪)

1618
01:50:40.800 --> 01:50:43.406
(CHUCKLES) I don't know, man.
You can have her.

1619
01:50:43.600 --> 01:50:47.604
She's one of these real Greenwich
Village phonies, if you ask me.

1620
01:50:47.760 --> 01:50:49.524
WOMAN: I'm gonna remember that, Charles.

1621
01:50:49.680 --> 01:50:51.523
CHARLES: Where are you going, Ted?

1622
01:50:51.680 --> 01:50:53.648
Stick around a minute.

1623
01:50:55.480 --> 01:50:58.006
WOMAN: You're Phil's friend, aren't you?

1624
01:50:58.200 --> 01:51:00.441
I am, yes. And Dannie's.

1625
01:51:00.600 --> 01:51:03.490
Aren't you gonna ask me how I knew that?

1626
01:51:03.640 --> 01:51:06.405
Aren't most people here Phil's friends?

1627
01:51:06.560 --> 01:51:09.643
I can see why Phil speaks so highly of you.

1628
01:51:09.840 --> 01:51:11.410
Can you?

1629
01:51:11.560 --> 01:51:13.801
(♪♪♪)

1630
01:51:13.960 --> 01:51:16.930
(INDISTINCT CHATTER CONTINUES IN DISTANCE)

1631
01:51:20.240 --> 01:51:21.969
(KNOCKING)

1632
01:51:22.120 --> 01:51:24.487
Sorry, I'll be out in a minute.

1633
01:51:26.000 --> 01:51:28.048
(♪♪♪)

1634
01:51:31.200 --> 01:51:33.567
(LOW, INDISTINCT CHATTER)

1635
01:51:39.000 --> 01:51:43.641
MAN (ON TV): Davidson's getting
her ready to go back to San Francisco.

1636
01:51:43.800 --> 01:51:45.768
MAN 2: What's he been doing to her?

1637
01:51:45.960 --> 01:51:48.406
- MAN: Praying.
- MAN 2: Praying?

1638
01:51:48.560 --> 01:51:50.608
MAN: Praying.

1639
01:51:50.760 --> 01:51:52.728
(♪♪♪)

1640
01:51:57.080 --> 01:51:58.650
(INDISTINCT CONVERSATION,
LAUGHING IN DISTANCE)

1641
01:51:58.800 --> 01:52:00.609
MAN: Maybe, yes.

1642
01:52:00.760 --> 01:52:03.809
I think that'd be a great idea. (LAUGHS)

1643
01:52:03.960 --> 01:52:05.883
MAN 2: As bad as Boston?

1644
01:52:07.120 --> 01:52:10.602
(TRAIN WHEELS CLACKING)

1645
01:52:10.760 --> 01:52:12.762
(TRAIN BRAKES SQUEAKING)

1646
01:52:15.080 --> 01:52:17.924
MAN: Well, next year
you're gonna have to come out.

1647
01:52:18.080 --> 01:52:19.844
Come out for the summer.

1648
01:52:20.000 --> 01:52:22.480
WOMAN: Of course, no one dares
to tell them otherwise.

1649
01:52:22.640 --> 01:52:24.051
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

1650
01:52:24.200 --> 01:52:26.885
MAN: He does that all the time.
He's a blowhard.

1651
01:52:27.080 --> 01:52:29.287
MAN 2: Right? There's no more direct...

1652
01:52:29.440 --> 01:52:31.204
MAN 3: About how much time would you say?

1653
01:52:31.360 --> 01:52:32.930
Do you have a reservation?

1654
01:52:33.080 --> 01:52:34.525
- Sorry, I'm looking for someone.
- W...

1655
01:52:34.680 --> 01:52:36.682
Ma'am, I can't seat you... without a party.

1656
01:52:36.880 --> 01:52:38.120
- MAN: Good night, Sammy!
- Mr. Ellsworth.

1657
01:52:38.280 --> 01:52:40.203
MAN: Good night, Dennis. Thanks again.

1658
01:52:40.360 --> 01:52:42.442
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

1659
01:52:44.880 --> 01:52:47.121
MAN: Uh, question.

1660
01:52:47.280 --> 01:52:48.645
MAN 2: Yeah?

1661
01:53:00.400 --> 01:53:02.562
(♪♪♪)

1662
01:53:15.360 --> 01:53:17.249
(SIGHS SOFTLY)

1663
01:53:17.400 --> 01:53:19.368
(♪♪♪)

1664
01:53:43.720 --> 01:53:45.643
(♪♪♪)

1665
01:54:10.120 --> 01:54:12.088
(♪♪♪)

1666
01:54:32.240 --> 01:54:34.163
(♪♪♪)

1667
01:55:05.000 --> 01:55:07.048
(♪♪♪)

1668
01:55:31.640 --> 01:55:33.608
(♪♪♪)

1669
01:56:04.680 --> 01:56:07.001
(♪♪♪)

1670
01:56:37.480 --> 01:56:39.801
(♪♪♪)

1671
01:57:09.440 --> 01:57:11.761
(♪♪♪)

1672
01:57:41.600 --> 01:57:43.921
(♪♪♪)

1673
01:58:13.560 --> 01:58:15.927
(♪♪♪)

1674
01:58:19.960 --> 01:58:21.883
(MUSIC ENDS)
